OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026", "title": "A Low-Space Algorithm for Worst-Case Subset Sum", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "138", "family_title": "Subset Sum in \\(O(2^{0.49n})\\) time", "abstract_html": "We give a uniform classical randomized decision algorithm for worst-case Subset Sum. Under every fixed polynomial bound on input-integer bit length, it uses \\(\\mathop{\\mathrm{poly}}\\nolimits (n)2^{n/2}\\) time and ordinary \\(O(2^{n/5})\\) writable words of \\(O(n+b)\\) bits, where <i>b</i> is the largest input bit length. Both resource bounds hold on every execution. The error is one-sided: the algorithm always rejects unsolvable instances and accepts each solvable instance with probability at least 2/3.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">An instance of Subset Sum consists of positive integers \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\), with \\(n\\ge 2\\), and a nonnegative integer target \\(t\\). Repeated values are allowed, and the empty subset is allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  b=\\max\\!\\left(1,\\left\\lceil\\log_2\\!\\left(1+\\max(t,a_1,\\ldots,a_n)\\right)\\right\\rceil\\right),\n  \\qquad u=n+b+2,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  w=\\left\\lceil4\\bigl(n+b+\\log_2(n+2)\\bigr)\\right\\rceil.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The input is in read-only random-access memory, one integer per word. Unit-cost operations are word reads and writes, indirect addressing, comparisons, addition, subtraction, multiplication, integer quotient and remainder with nonzero divisor, bitwise Boolean operations, and shifts. Each operation has constantly many \\(w\\)-bit inputs and outputs. Arithmetic exceeding these limits must be implemented and charged as multiple operations.</p><p class=\"li\">A fresh independent uniform \\(w\\)-bit random word costs one operation.</p><p class=\"li\">Every writable register, address, counter, stack, table, seed, and retained random value counts toward working space. Discarded randomness cannot subsequently be accessed. Only the read-only input and the fixed finite program are uncharged. There is no random oracle, advice, or externally supplied table depending on \\(n\\) or the input.</p><p class=\"li\">All preprocessing, input accesses, table construction, and random sampling count toward running time. Space is the maximum number of writable words simultaneously in use.</p><p class=\"li\">A fixed polynomial-bit convention chooses a positive integer \\(c\\) and considers inputs with \\(b\\le n^c\\). The algorithm is not given \\(c\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a single uniform classical randomized algorithm in this model that never answers YES incorrectly and answers each YES instance correctly with probability at least \\(2/3\\). For every fixed positive integer \\(c\\), there are a constant \\(A_c&gt;0\\) and positive integers \\(k_c,N_c\\) such that, whenever \\(n\\ge N_c\\) and \\(b\\le n^c\\), every outcome of the algorithm’s randomness uses at most\n\\[\nA_c n^{k_c}2^{n/2}\\quad\\text{operations}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nA_c(n+b+2)^{15}2^{0.199n}\\quad\\text{writable words}.\n\\]\nThe algorithm does not depend on \\(c\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the single uniform algorithm of Proposition 8.</p><p>For a main-branch YES input, let \\(\\mu\\) be its size-\\(s\\) mean deficiency. If \\(\\mu\\ge.00765n\\), Proposition 3 gives an absolute positive success probability for the compressed-block trial. If \\(\\mu&lt;.00765n\\), Proposition 7 gives success probability at least \\(u^{-110}\\) for the representation trial. Both trials run in every round; the algorithm need not determine which case applies.</p><p>For sufficiently large main-branch inputs, each round therefore succeeds with probability at least \\(u^{-110}\\), also conditional on previous rounds failing, because each round uses fresh randomness and reset state. After \\(u^{120}\\) rounds, the miss probability is at most\n\\[\n(1-u^{-110})^{u^{120}}\\le e^{-u^{10}}&lt;1/3.\n\\]\nEvery YES is an exact valid match, and the sign-toggle identity yields an original solution. Thus no NO instance is accepted. The exceptional branch is exact, so correctness holds on every individual input.</p><p>Proposition 8 gives, simultaneously on every main-branch random outcome,\n\\[\nO(u^{620}2^{\\lceil n/2\\rceil}+\\operatorname{poly}(u))\n\\]\noperations and\n\\[\nO(u^{15}2^{.199n})\n\\]\nwritable words.</p><p>Fix any positive integer \\(c\\). All sufficiently large inputs with \\(b\\le n^c\\) satisfy the main-branch guard, and \\(u=n+b+2=\\operatorname{poly}_c(n)\\). Absorb the resulting polynomial time factors and absolute implementation constants into \\(A_c n^{k_c}\\), enlarging \\(N_c\\) if necessary. Since \\(2^{\\lceil n/2\\rceil}=O(2^{n/2})\\), this yields the stated time bound and the stated \\(A_cu^{15}2^{.199n}\\) space bound.</p><p>The program, its threshold, its sampling limits, and its repetition schedule are independent of \\(c\\). This proves Theorem 1.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the input model, polynomial-bit convention, and algorithm of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a real number \\(\\rho&gt;0.199\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Under every fixed polynomial-bit convention \\(b\\le n^c\\), the algorithm has ordinary\n\\[\nO_{c,\\rho}(2^{\\rho n})\n\\]\nword space, with the time and correctness guarantees of Theorem 1. In particular, it has ordinary\n\\[\nO_c(2^{n/5})\n\\]\nword space.</p><p>At the endpoint \\(\\rho=0.199\\), the polynomial factor in Theorem 1 remains.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Theorem 1, under a fixed convention \\(b\\le n^c\\),\n\\[\nu^{15}=(n+b+2)^{15}=\\operatorname{poly}_c(n).\n\\]\nFor each fixed \\(\\rho&gt;.199\\), this polynomial factor is absorbed by\n\\[\n2^{(\\rho-.199)n}.\n\\]\nHence the same algorithm has ordinary \\(O_{c,\\rho}(2^{\\rho n})\\) word space, retaining Theorem 1’s simultaneous per-execution time bound and one-sided correctness.</p><p>Taking \\(\\rho=1/5=.2\\) gives ordinary \\(O_c(2^{n/5})\\) word space. At \\(\\rho=.199\\), the polynomial factor is not absorbed and remains as stated.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Counting and exact-sampling tools.", "statement": "<p>**Counting and exact-sampling tools. Original components: Lemmas 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, and 7.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All logarithms are base two unless written \\(\\ln\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For arbitrary fixed, possibly signed, weights on \\(n\\) indices and a domain \\(V\\), let \\(N(V)\\) be the number of distinct integer subset sums on \\(V\\), and define\n  \\[\n  d(V)=|V|-\\log N(V).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For input parameters \\(n,b\\), put \\(u=n+b+2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define binary entropy by\n  \\[\n  h(x)=-x\\log x-(1-x)\\log(1-x),\n  \\]\n  with continuous endpoint values, and put \\(h_*=h(1/4)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For an integer \\(4\\le\\ell\\le n\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal P_\\ell=\\{p\\text{ prime}:p&lt;2^\\ell,\\ 4\\ell p\\ge2^\\ell\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A bounded rejection sampler makes at most \\(n^3\\) attempts and reports failure if none accepts.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following tools hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Deficiency is nonnegative, invariant under individual sign changes, inclusion-monotone, and superadditive on disjoint domains. Replacing one index in a fixed-size domain changes deficiency by at most one. For a uniform size-\\(v\\) domain \\(V\\),\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Var}(d(V))\\le v.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For integers \\(0\\le k\\le v\\),\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2^{vh(k/v)}}{v+1}\\le\\binom vk\\le2^{vh(k/v)},\n   \\]\n   with the stated endpoint convention, and\n   \\[\n   h_*&lt;.8114,\\qquad h(1/3)&lt;14/15,\\qquad h(1/6)&gt;5/8.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The set \\(\\mathcal P_\\ell\\) has at least \\(2^\\ell/(4\\ell)\\) elements. For every fixed nonzero integer \\(\\Delta\\) with \\(|\\Delta|&lt;2^{2u}\\),\n   \\[\n   \\Pr_{p\\in\\mathcal P_\\ell}[p\\mid\\Delta]\\le8u^2\\,2^{-\\ell}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The range and prime samplers described in Lemma 1.4 have exact uniform output laws on acceptance, deterministic attempt limits, and the coupling guarantees stated there.</p>", "proof": "<p>The deficiency assertions and their concentration consequence are Lemma 1.1. The exact binomial and numerical entropy estimates are Lemma 1.2. Lemma 1.3 establishes the prime population and collision estimates. Lemma 1.4 implements the sampling interface with bounded work and the stated coupling guarantees.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1 (Deficiency)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The weights on the \\(n\\) indices are arbitrary fixed integers and may be signed.</p><p class=\"li\">For each domain \\(V\\), define \\(N(V)\\) and \\(d(V)\\) as in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be uniform among the size-\\(v\\) domains, where \\(0\\le v\\le n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Deficiency is nonnegative, invariant under sign changes of individual weights, inclusion-monotone, and superadditive on disjoint domains. Replacing one index in a domain of fixed size changes its deficiency by at most one. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(d(V))\\le v.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>There are \\(2^{|V|}\\) subsets, so \\(N(V)\\le2^{|V|}\\). Adding one index multiplies the number of sums by a factor between one and two. Its addition therefore increases deficiency by a quantity in \\([0,1]\\), proving inclusion monotonicity.</p><p>For disjoint \\(V,W\\), every sum on their union is a sum of one value from each domain, so\n\\[\nN(V\\cup W)\\le N(V)N(W).\n\\]\nTaking logarithms gives\n\\[\nd(V\\cup W)\\ge d(V)+d(W).\n\\]</p><p>For sign invariance, let \\(F\\subseteq V\\) be the negated indices. Toggling \\(F\\) in a chosen subset is a bijection on all subsets of \\(V\\). The new weight of a subset is the old weight of its toggle minus the old weight of \\(F\\), so the set of sum values is merely translated.</p><p>Two equal-sized domains differing in one index are extensions of their common part. Each extension increases deficiency by a quantity in \\([0,1]\\), which proves the replacement bound.</p><p>For the variance, expose a uniform ordered size-\\(v\\) sample without replacement. Let \\(M_r\\) be the conditional expectation of the final deficiency after the first \\(r\\) positions, \\(0\\le r\\le v\\). Given the first \\(r-1\\) positions, couple the remaining completions for two possible next indices by transposing those indices. The final domains then agree or differ in one index. Their deficiencies, and hence their conditional means, differ by at most one. Thus all possible values of \\(M_r\\) given the past lie in an interval of length at most one, and\n\\[\n|M_r-M_{r-1}|\\le1.\n\\]\nOrthogonality of martingale differences gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(d(V))\n=\\sum_{r=1}^v\\mathbb E(M_r-M_{r-1})^2\n\\le v.\n\\]\nThe empty-sample case is immediate.</p><p>Consequently, for every fixed \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), a deviation of \\(\\epsilon n\\) from the size-specific mean has probability \\(O(1/n)\\), by Chebyshev’s inequality. This concerns a random domain in a fixed input and assumes no distribution of the numerical input values.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.2 (Elementary entropy bounds)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v,k\\) be integers with \\(0\\le k\\le v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(h\\) and \\(h_*\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(v=k=0\\), interpret \\(vh(k/v)\\) as zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac{2^{vh(k/v)}}{v+1}\\le\\binom vk\\le2^{vh(k/v)}.\n\\]\nEntropy is concave, symmetric about \\(1/2\\), and increasing on \\([0,1/2]\\). Moreover,\n\\[\nh_*&lt;.8114,\\qquad h(1/3)&lt;14/15,\\qquad h(1/6)&gt;5/8.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(0&lt;k&lt;v\\), consider the binomial distribution with success probability \\(k/v\\). The probability of exactly \\(k\\) successes is\n\\[\n\\binom vk2^{-vh(k/v)}.\n\\]\nIt is at most one and at least \\(1/(v+1)\\): adjacent probability ratios show that \\(k\\) is a mode, and there are \\(v+1\\) possible outcomes. This gives both estimates. The cases \\(k=0,v\\) and \\(v=0\\) are immediate. Differentiation gives the asserted monotonicity and concavity; symmetry follows from the formula for \\(h\\).</p><p>For the strict numerical inequalities,\n\\[\nh_*=2-\\frac34\\log3.\n\\]\nWe have\n\\[\n\\frac{3^{12}}{2^{19}}&gt;1+\\frac1{74},\n\\]\nwhose fourth power exceeds\n\\[\n1+\\frac4{74}=\\frac{39}{37}&gt;\\frac{256}{243}.\n\\]\nHence \\(3^{53}&gt;2^{84}\\), so\n\\[\n\\log3&gt;\\frac{84}{53}&gt;1.5848,\n\\]\ngiving \\(h_*&lt;.8114\\).</p><p>Next,\n\\[\nh(1/3)=\\log3-\\frac23,\n\\]\nand \\(3^5&lt;2^8\\), so\n\\[\nh(1/3)&lt;\\frac85-\\frac23=\\frac{14}{15}.\n\\]</p><p>Finally, \\(6^2&gt;2^5\\) and \\((6/5)^4&gt;2\\) imply\n\\[\nh(1/6)\n=\\frac16\\log6+\\frac56\\log(6/5)\n&gt;\\frac16\\cdot\\frac52+\\frac56\\cdot\\frac14\n=\\frac58.\n\\]\nThus none of the three bounds relies on floating-point optimization.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.3 (Prime factors)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(4\\le\\ell\\le n\\) be an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal P_\\ell=\\{p\\text{ prime}:p&lt;2^\\ell,\\ 4\\ell p\\ge2^\\ell\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Delta\\ne0\\) be a fixed integer with \\(|\\Delta|&lt;2^{2u}\\), where \\(u=n+b+2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Sample \\(p\\) uniformly from \\(\\mathcal P_\\ell\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The set \\(\\mathcal P_\\ell\\) has at least \\(2^\\ell/(4\\ell)\\) elements, and\n\\[\n\\Pr[p\\mid\\Delta]\\le8u^2\\,2^{-\\ell}.\n\\]\nIn particular, the estimate applies to every nonzero actual-weight difference used by the two trials below, for sufficiently large main-branch inputs.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(X=2^\\ell\\). The central binomial coefficient obeys\n\\[\n\\binom X{X/2}\\ge\\frac{2^X}{X+1}.\n\\]\nIn its factorial formula, the exponent of a prime \\(p\\) is\n\\[\n\\sum_{r\\ge1}\n\\left(\\left\\lfloor\\frac X{p^r}\\right\\rfloor\n-2\\left\\lfloor\\frac{X/2}{p^r}\\right\\rfloor\\right).\n\\]\nEach summand is zero or one, and at most \\(\\lfloor\\log_pX\\rfloor\\) are nonzero. Thus each prime contributes a factor at most \\(X\\) to the binomial coefficient.</p><p>If \\(\\pi(X)\\) is the number of primes at most \\(X\\), taking logarithms gives\n\\[\n\\ell\\pi(X)\\ge X-\\log(X+1)\\ge X/2\n\\qquad(X\\ge16).\n\\]\nRemove primes below \\(X/(4\\ell)\\). There are at most \\(X/(4\\ell)\\) integers in that range, so at least \\(X/(4\\ell)\\) primes remain. The upper endpoint \\(X\\) is composite, proving the cardinality bound.</p><p>An integer \\(\\Delta\\) with \\(0&lt;|\\Delta|&lt;2^{2u}\\) has at most \\(2u\\) distinct prime divisors: the product of \\(r\\) distinct primes is at least \\(2^r\\). Dividing by the number of available primes and using \\(\\ell\\le n\\le u\\) gives the collision bound.</p><p>Actual weights and their relevant differences have magnitude \\(O(n2^b)\\). The constructions sum each indexed weight at most twice, even before rejecting an overlapping tuple. They therefore meet \\(|\\Delta|&lt;2^{2u}\\) for sufficiently large main-branch inputs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 7.1 (Bounded samplers)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the word model in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">A range draw specifies \\(1\\le D&lt;2^w\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A prime draw specifies an integer \\(4\\le\\ell\\le n\\) and the set \\(\\mathcal P_\\ell\\) of Lemma 1.3.</p><p class=\"li\">Each draw is allowed at most \\(n^3\\) rejection attempts.</p><p class=\"li\">In the trials below, every requested prime parameter satisfies \\(\\ell\\le.258n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The four separating families used below each contain at most \\(10\\cdot2^g+1\\) subsets of a \\(g\\)-element block, where \\(g\\le.06n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A range draw either returns an integer in \\(\\{0,\\ldots,D-1\\}\\) or reports failure. Conditional on the preceding history and on acceptance of this call, its output is uniform. It couples to an ideal draw with failure probability at most \\(2^{-n^3}\\).</p><p>A prime draw has the uniform law on \\(\\mathcal P_\\ell\\) conditional on acceptance and couples to an ideal draw with failure probability at most \\(e^{-n^2/4}\\).</p><p>A full trial, including permutations, signs, residues, and all four separating families, couples to its ideal experiment with total failure probability \\(\\exp(-\\Omega(n^2))\\). All constants are absolute, and all permitted attempts are included in the deterministic work bounds. Prime sampling costs at most \\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)2^{.129n}\\) operations per call, including its attempts.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(D=1\\), return zero without randomness. Otherwise take \\(\\lceil\\log D\\rceil\\) low bits of a fresh random word and reject values at least \\(D\\). Each value in the range has the same acceptance probability, and an attempt succeeds with probability at least \\(1/2\\). The first accepted value is therefore uniform, including conditional on accepting within \\(n^3\\) attempts. Comparing the same attempts with an ideal repeat-until-success sampler gives failure probability at most \\(2^{-n^3}\\). Computing the bit length and each attempt takes polynomially many charged word operations.</p><p>Uniform permutations use successive range draws among the remaining positions; a uniform fixed-size subset takes the required initial part of such a permutation. Sign masks use fresh unbiased bits.</p><p>For a prime, draw \\(\\ell\\) low bits, reject integers below two or outside \\(4\\ell p\\ge2^\\ell\\), and test primality by deterministic trial division. Every prime in \\(\\mathcal P_\\ell\\) has the same probability of being drawn. Lemma 1.3 gives acceptance probability at least \\(1/(4\\ell)\\), so the attempt limit fails with probability at most\n\\[\n(1-1/(4\\ell))^{n^3}\n\\le e^{-n^3/(4\\ell)}\n\\le e^{-n^2/4}.\n\\]\nThe result is uniform conditional on this call accepting. In every application \\(\\ell\\le.258n\\), so trial division costs at most \\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)2^{.129n}\\) operations per attempt, and \\(n^3\\) attempts preserve this form of bound.</p><p>The four families are the only exponentially numerous sampled objects. Each has at most \\(10\\cdot2^g+1\\) sets, each generated by at most \\(g\\) range calls. All other permutations, guesses, and residues use polynomially many range calls, and there are constantly many prime calls. Thus each trial uses at most \\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)2^g\\) range calls.</p><p>The failure bounds hold conditional on every history, including adaptively chosen ranges. Summing over the bounded number of calls gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{poly}(n)2^g2^{-n^3}+O(1)e^{-n^2/4}\n=\\exp(-\\Omega(n^2)).\n\\]\nThis bounds the event that bounded and ideal executions first diverge at an exhausted sampler. One does not condition the entire ideal experiment on every sampler succeeding. Only current sampler state is retained, and no uncounted random tape is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Small-state enumeration and bounded dictionaries.", "statement": "<p>**Small-state enumeration and bounded dictionaries. Original component: Lemma 2.4 and Section 2.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">An item has an actual, possibly signed, integer weight and constantly many index masks. Each item occupies constantly many words. Repeated occurrences remain separate even when their fields agree.</p><p class=\"li\">Array indices have \\(O(n)\\) bits. Arithmetic, comparisons, and indexed access on the item fields take \\(O(1)\\) word operations.</p><p class=\"li\">For an item \\(a\\), write \\(\\omega(a)\\) for its actual weight.</p><p class=\"li\">A dictionary key consists of a fixed-width signed actual weight, followed when required by mask bits. Its total width is \\(O(u)\\), where \\(u=n+b+2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B\\ge1\\) is a tie-buffer capacity, and \\(C\\ge1\\) is a capacity on distinct dictionary entries.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Filtered pair streams and their equal-key joins can be implemented with the initialization, advance, storage, and stopped-execution bounds of Lemma 2.1. Actual-weight pair streams can also count distinct sums without storing their Cartesian product. Deterministic dictionaries can enforce capacity \\(C\\), deduplicate the full specified key, and use \\(O(u(C+1))\\) words, with \\(O(u)\\) operations per search or insertion.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 supplies sorted modular streams and stopped tie joins. Lemma 2.2 supplies actual-weight streams and deterministic capacity enforcement. When combined stream storage is \\(O(B)\\), the \\(Z\\)-dependent term of (5) is \\(O(nZ)\\), before separately charging work performed on those matches. Buffer and stream-length terms remain separate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.4 (Filtered streams and tie joins)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A_1,A_2\\) be stored constant-word item arrays satisfying Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\mid P\\) be positive integers, and let \\(c,v\\) be specified integer residues.</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize all residues modulo \\(P\\) to \\(\\{0,\\ldots,P-1\\}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The pairs \\((a,b)\\in A_1\\times A_2\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\omega(a)+\\omega(b)\\equiv c\\pmod p\n\\]\ncan be streamed in nondecreasing order of either\n\\[\n(\\omega(a)+\\omega(b))\\bmod P\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n(v-\\omega(a)-\\omega(b))\\bmod P.\n\\]\nInitialization costs \\(O(n(|A_1|+|A_2|+1))\\) operations, each subsequent advance costs \\(O(n)\\), and the state and input arrays use \\(O(|A_1|+|A_2|+1)\\) words.</p><p>For two such streams of lengths \\(N_1,N_2\\), let their combined states and arrays occupy at most \\(S\\) words, and let \\(Z\\) count all pairs in their equal-key Cartesian products. They can report all such pairs using \\(O(S+B)\\) words and, apart from initialization and work on reported pairs,\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(B+n(N_1+N_2+Z)+\\frac SB Z\\right)\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\noperations.</p><p>If \\(S\\le\\operatorname{poly}(n)B\\), this is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{poly}(n)(B+N_1+N_2+Z).\n\\]\nThe same upper bounds hold for an execution stopped partway, with \\(Z\\) still counting all potential matches. No coprimality assumption is needed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Sort \\(A_2\\) lexicographically by residue modulo \\(p\\), then residue modulo \\(P\\). For a fixed \\(a\\in A_1\\), binary search finds the contiguous slice having residue \\(c-\\omega(a)\\pmod p\\). This slice contains exactly the eligible partners and is ordered by their \\(P\\)-residues, including repetitions. Omit an empty row.</p><p>For a direct key, put \\(r=\\omega(a)\\bmod P\\). Start at the first slice entry whose residue is at least \\(P-r\\); if none exists, start at the beginning. Traverse forward cyclically for exactly the slice length. Entries at or above the pivot give wrapped values\n\\[\nr+(\\omega(b)\\bmod P)-P\n\\]\nfirst, followed by unwrapped values\n\\[\nr+(\\omega(b)\\bmod P).\n\\]\nBoth portions are nondecreasing, and all values in the first portion are smaller. This includes \\(r=0\\), when the pivot search returns the beginning.</p><p>For a complementary key, put \\(K=(v-\\omega(a))\\bmod P\\). Start at the last entry whose residue is at most \\(K\\); if none exists, start at the last slice entry. Traverse in reverse cyclic order for exactly one slice length. Residues at most \\(K\\) give\n\\[\nK-(\\omega(b)\\bmod P)\n\\]\nin nondecreasing order. The residues above \\(K\\) follow, giving\n\\[\nK-(\\omega(b)\\bmod P)+P,\n\\]\nalso in nondecreasing order. Choosing the first pivot in the direct case and last pivot in the complementary case preserves every repeated residue.</p><p>Merge the rows with a min-heap having at most one current pair per item of \\(A_1\\). A row stores slice endpoints, current position, and remaining length. Comparison sorting, binary searches, and heap operations have depth \\(O(n)\\), since indices have \\(O(n)\\) bits. This proves the stream bounds. Common factors and repeated prime factors cause no difficulty. Empty arrays give empty streams.</p><p>To join the streams, advance past unequal keys. At an equal key, read up to \\(B\\) second-stream items into a reusable buffer. If these exhaust its tie group, pair the buffer with every first-stream item having that key. No state is copied. Reuse only the buffer’s current length, without clearing \\(B\\) positions for a short group.</p><p>If the second tie group is longer, save its mutable generator state immediately after the first \\(B\\) items. The snapshot includes lookahead, heap, row cursors, and control state, but shares immutable arrays. For each first-stream item of that key, report its pairs with the buffer, restore the saved state when necessary, and replay the remaining suffix. After the final replay, the second stream is beyond the tie group.</p><p>Each save or restore costs \\(O(S)\\). It is made only for a first item with at least \\(B\\) potential partners, and only constantly many times per such item. Total copying cost is \\(O(SZ/B)\\). Replayed advances are charged to matching pairs; other advances are charged to original stream lengths. Adding the \\(O(n)\\) advance cost and optional \\(O(B)\\) buffer allocation gives (5).</p><p>If stopped just after a snapshot or restore, the same charge remains valid because \\(Z\\) counts potential pairs, including unreached pairs. One snapshot, one buffer, and both generators give the space bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "the actual-weight stream observation following Lemma 2.4, and Section 2.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the item representation and key-width assumptions of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">A dictionary has distinct-entry capacity \\(C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A signed weight uses canonical nonnegative zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>An actual-weight pair stream can enumerate all pairs in sorted actual-weight order and count distinct resulting weights using only the stream state and one remembered last weight.</p><p>A deterministic dictionary can search or insert a key in \\(O(u)\\) operations, introducing at most \\(O(u)\\) trie nodes per new key. It reports overflow when a new key would make its entry array exceed \\(C\\). Including nodes transiently allocated for the first rejected key, it uses \\(O(u(C+1))\\) words.</p><p>In the applications below, raw item generation costs \\(O(n^2)\\) per item, sorting and heap work cost \\(O(n)\\) per item or advance, and dictionary actions cost \\(O(u)\\) per key.</p>", "proof": "<p>For an actual-weight stream, sort the second array by actual weight, add each first-array weight to that sorted row, and heap-merge the rows. Read the resulting stream while remembering only the last weight. This counts distinct values without storing the Cartesian product.</p><p>For a dictionary, use a binary trie on the fixed-width signed actual weight, followed by relevant mask bits when a mask is part of the key. A key has \\(O(u)\\) bits, so search or insertion takes \\(O(u)\\) operations and adds at most \\(O(u)\\) nodes, with constantly many words per node.</p><p>If later array access is needed, retain a separate append-only array of distinct entries. Duplicates change neither its length nor content. On encountering a new key that would increase the array beyond \\(C\\), report overflow. Even if its nodes have been allocated, there are only \\(O(u(C+1))\\) nodes.</p><p>A weight-only key is appropriate when the compressed domains are disjoint. A weight–mask key is necessary when compatibility depends on shared indices. No cost is inferred merely from the number of distinct retained entries: occurrence generation, stream scans, tie replay, and dictionary actions are charged separately.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Compressed-block trial.", "statement": "<p>**Compressed-block trial. Original label: Lemma 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the input and word model of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  u=n+b+2,\\quad m=8\\lfloor3n/100\\rfloor,\\quad s=m/2,\\quad g=m/4,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  C=2^{\\lfloor.199n\\rfloor},\\qquad B=2^{\\lfloor.19n\\rfloor}.\n  \\tag{1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The main branch consists of inputs satisfying\n  \\[\n  n\\ge n_0,\\qquad u\\le2^{\\lfloor n/10^9\\rfloor},\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n  for one sufficiently large absolute integer \\(n_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mu\\) be the mean of \\(d(V)\\) over uniform size-\\(s\\) domains \\(V\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose seven consecutive disjoint size-\\(s\\) blocks \\(K_1,\\ldots,K_7\\) from a uniform random permutation. Enumerate, sort, and compact each block’s subset sums into a distinct-weight list \\(D_i\\). Continue only if all lists have at most \\(2^a\\) entries, where\n  \\[\n  a=\\lfloor s-.0075n\\rfloor.\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Among remaining indices, assign\n  \\[\n  r_R=\\lfloor.05n\\rfloor,\\qquad r_L=n-7s-r_R\n  \\]\n  to right and left raw domains. Split the right domain into \\(V_{R1},V_{R2}\\) as equally as possible; call the left domain \\(V_L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal S(V)\\) be the full list of indexed subset-sum occurrences on \\(V\\), retaining repetitions. For lists \\(A,D\\), let \\(A\\oplus D\\) contain one sum occurrence for each pair.</p><p class=\"li\">Define four groups from their two stored lists:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n  \\text{group}&amp;\\text{domain}&amp;\\text{first list}&amp;\\text{second list}\\\\ \\hline\n  L_1&amp;K_5\\cup K_6&amp;D_5&amp;D_6\\\\\n  L_2&amp;K_7\\cup V_L&amp;D_7&amp;\\mathcal S(V_L)\\\\\n  R_1&amp;K_1\\cup K_2\\cup V_{R1}&amp;D_1&amp;D_2\\oplus\\mathcal S(V_{R1})\\\\\n  R_2&amp;K_3\\cup K_4\\cup V_{R2}&amp;D_3&amp;D_4\\oplus\\mathcal S(V_{R2})\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Sample \\(q\\) uniformly from \\(\\mathcal P_{\\lfloor.249n\\rfloor}\\). For each \\(z=0,\\ldots,q-1\\):</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. Join \\(L_1\\), ordered by weight modulo \\(q\\), with \\(L_2\\), ordered by \\(z-\\text{weight}\\) modulo \\(q\\), using Proposition 2 with \\(p=1,P=q\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. Insert resulting actual left weights into a weight-only dictionary of capacity \\(C\\). Skip \\(z\\) on overflow or an empty completed dictionary.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. Otherwise join \\(R_1,R_2\\) for total residue \\(t-z\\pmod q\\). For each actual right weight \\(y\\), search for \\(t-y\\) in the left dictionary. Return YES on a hit.</p><p class=\"li\">Sampling failure fails the trial.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large main-branch inputs, this trial uses at most\n\\[\nu^{15}2^{n/2}\n\\]\nword operations on every random outcome, including bounded sampling, and\n\\[\nO\\bigl(u(C+1)+B+2^s+2^{.14n}\\bigr)\n\\]\nwritable words. Every YES is correct. If the instance is solvable and\n\\[\n\\mu\\ge.00765n,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nthe trial succeeds with probability bounded below by an absolute positive constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 gives the deterministic resource bounds and one-sided correctness. Lemma 3.2 gives success in the high-mean case. The algorithm does not compute \\(\\mu\\); this hypothesis selects the success analysis, not an executable branch.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Deterministic work, space, and one-sided correctness in the proof of original Lemma 3.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Deterministic work, space, and one-sided correctness in the proof of original Lemma 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use exactly the compressed-block trial and parameters of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Resource bounds include failed size tests, stopped joins, and bounded sampling.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every returned YES is correct. On every random outcome, the trial uses at most \\(u^{15}2^{n/2}\\) operations and\n\\[\nO\\bigl(u(C+1)+B+2^s+2^{.14n}\\bigr)\n\\]\nwritable words, for sufficiently large main-branch inputs.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every retained block weight has a subset witness in its block. The four group domains are disjoint and partition the input indices. Thus an exact complementary left–right match gives an indexed subset of weight \\(t\\); witnesses need not be retained for a decision algorithm.</p><p>Assume the seven size tests pass. Put\n\\[\n\\delta=.12n-s\\in[0,4),\\qquad\n\\epsilon=.05n-r_R\\in[0,1).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nr_L=.11n+7\\delta+\\epsilon,\\qquad a\\le.1125n-\\delta.\n\\]\nEach stored product list on the right has at most\n\\[\n2^{a+\\lceil r_R/2\\rceil}\\le2^{.1375n+1/2}\n\\]\noccurrences. The left raw list has at most \\(2^{.11n+29}\\) occurrences, and each \\(D_i\\) has at most \\(2^{.1125n}\\) entries. Therefore every stored sublist has at most \\(2^{.14n}\\) items for sufficiently large \\(n\\).</p><p>Let \\(N_{L1},N_{L2},N_{R1},N_{R2}\\) be group-stream lengths, counting occurrences. Then\n\\[\nN_{L1}\\le2^{2a}\\le2^{.225n},\\qquad\nN_{L2}\\le2^{a+r_L}\\le2^{.2225n+25},\n\\]\n\\[\nN_{Ri}\\le2^{2a+r_{Ri}}\\le2^{.25n+1/2}.\n\\]\nTheir complete-combination counts satisfy\n\\[\nN_{R1}N_{R2}\\le2^{4a+r_R}\n\\le2^{.5n-4\\delta-\\epsilon}\\le2^{n/2},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nN_{L1}N_{L2}\\le2^{3a+r_L}\n\\le2^{.4475n+4\\delta+\\epsilon}\n&lt;2^{.4475n+17}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]</p><p>Each complete left combination has exactly one residue modulo \\(q\\), and each complete right combination has exactly one complementary residue. Across the entire residue loop, potential equal-key Cartesian matches total at most\n\\[\nN_{L1}N_{L2}+N_{R1}N_{R2}.\n\\]\nThe stopped-join bound of Proposition 2 charges an overflowing join to these same potential counts, preserving the accounting for repetitions and replay.</p><p>Let \\(A\\) be the total size of stored sublists and\n\\[\nN=N_{L1}+N_{L2}+N_{R1}+N_{R2}.\n\\]\nRaw generation costs \\(O(n^2)\\) per item, sorting and heap work \\(O(n)\\) per item or advance, and trie actions \\(O(u)\\) per key. Group states occupy \\(O(2^{.14n})=O(B)\\) words. Even initializing \\(O(u(C+1))\\) trie space afresh at each residue, all nonsampling work is bounded by\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(\nu^3\\left[\n2^s+A+q\\bigl(N+B+u(C+1)+1\\bigr)\n+N_{L1}N_{L2}+N_{R1}N_{R2}\n\\right]\\right).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nThe \\(2^s\\) term pays for initial enumeration and compaction; the \\(q\\)-multiplied terms pay regeneration and initialization; the final products pay potential matches and dictionary actions.</p><p>Since \\(q&lt;2^{.249n}\\), the largest regenerated-stream exponent is \\(.249+.25=.499\\). Buffer and dictionary initialization have exponents \\(.439\\) and \\(.448\\). Equations (8)–(9) give total nonsampling work \\(O(u^4 2^{n/2})\\).</p><p>A permutation costs at most \\(O(n^4)\\) bounded range attempts and elementary operations. Proposition 1 bounds the prime search by \\(O(n^4 2^{.129n})\\). These smaller terms leave the total below \\(u^{15}2^{n/2}\\) for sufficiently large \\(n\\).</p><p>Initial enumerations use \\(O(2^s)\\) words, stored sublists and stream states use \\(O(2^{.14n})\\), tie replay uses \\(O(B)\\), and the dictionary uses \\(O(u(C+1))\\). Sorting uses linear auxiliary arrays. Permutation and loop state are absorbed. Overflow enforces this space bound on every outcome.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**High-mean success in the proof of original Lemma 3.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**High-mean success in the proof of original Lemma 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the compressed-block trial of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">The input is a YES instance and its size-\\(s\\) mean deficiency satisfies \\(\\mu\\ge.00765n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large main-branch inputs, the implemented compressed-block trial succeeds with probability bounded below by an absolute positive constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>Each \\(K_i\\) is marginally a uniform size-\\(s\\) domain. Lemma 1.1, Chebyshev’s inequality, and a union bound give\n\\[\n\\Pr[d(K_i)\\ge.00755n\\text{ for every }i]=1-O(1/n).\n\\]\nThese inequalities imply\n\\[\nN(K_i)\\le2^{s-.00755n}\\le2^a\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(n\\), so all size tests pass, including their floors.</p><p>Fix an original solution before sampling the partition. Conditional on a passing partition, let \\(x\\) be its left weight. There are fewer than \\(2^{.4475n+17}\\) distinct left weights. For each different weight \\(x'\\), Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\n\\Pr[x'\\equiv x\\pmod q]\n\\le8u^2 2^{-\\lfloor.249n\\rfloor}\n\\le u^3 2^{-.249n}.\n\\]\nIf \\(Y\\) counts other distinct weights in \\(x\\)’s residue class, then\n\\[\n\\mathbb EY\\le u^3 2^{.1985n+17}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(C\\ge2^{.199n-1}\\) and \\(\\log u\\le n/10^9\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathbb EY}{C}\n\\le2^{(3/10^9-.0005)n+18}=o(1).\n\\]\nMarkov’s inequality gives \\(Y+1\\le C\\) with probability tending to one. The complete dictionary for this residue retains \\(x\\), and the right solution weight gives a hit. Earlier residues only yield an earlier correct YES or are skipped.</p><p>The ideal experiment therefore succeeds with probability \\(1-o(1)\\). Proposition 1 gives negligible bounded-sampler coupling loss, and Lemma 3.1 keeps the completed trial below the operation cap used later. An absolute positive success probability remains.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Representation layout, filtered parent generation, and prefix accounting.", "statement": "<p>**Representation layout, filtered parent generation, and prefix accounting. Original components: Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2 and Sections 4.1–4.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the input model of Theorem 1 and the main-branch parameters\n  \\[\n  u=n+b+2,\\quad m=8\\lfloor3n/100\\rfloor,\\quad s=m/2,\\quad g=m/4,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  C=2^{\\lfloor.199n\\rfloor},\\quad B=2^{\\lfloor.19n\\rfloor},\n  \\quad n\\ge n_0,\\quad \\log u\\le n/10^9.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Select three disjoint size-\\(m\\) mixers uniformly from a permutation. Compute each distinct-sum count by storing its two size-\\(s\\) half lists and counting distinct values in an actual-weight pair stream.</p><p class=\"li\">Name a mixer with largest sum count \\(M_0\\); name the others \\(M_L,M_R\\) deterministically. Put\n  \\[\n  N_i=N(M_i),\\qquad d_i=m-\\log N_i\n  \\quad(i\\in\\{0,L,R\\}).\n  \\]\n  Continue only if\n  \\[\n  \\max_iN_i\\le(\\min_iN_i)2^{\\lfloor.0005n\\rfloor}.\n  \\tag{11}\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(d_0\\le d_L,d_R\\), and deficiency spread is at most \\(.0005n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a uniform subset \\(F\\) of all indices, negate its weights, and use the transformed target\n  \\[\n  t'=t-\\sum_{f\\in F}a_f,\n  \\tag{12}\n  \\]\n  with original weights in the displayed sum. Subsequent weights in this trial are signed.</p><p class=\"li\">Independently guess\n  \\[\n  k_i\\in\\{\\lceil m/6\\rceil,\\ldots,m/4\\}\n  \\]\n  uniformly, and put \\(\\lambda_i=k_i/m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Partition \\(M_0=T_1\\dot\\cup\\cdots\\dot\\cup T_4\\) uniformly with \\(|T_a|=g\\). Choose \\(c_1,\\ldots,c_4\\) as equally as possible, summing to \\(k_0\\), with a fixed remainder order. The left top-mask count in \\(T_a\\) is \\(c_a\\), and the right count is \\(g/2-c_a\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Within each parent, child 1 uses \\(T_1,T_2\\), and child 2 uses \\(T_3,T_4\\), one top block per leaf.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(j=L,R\\), independently partition\n  \\[\n  M_j=Q_{j1}\\dot\\cup Q_{j2},\\qquad |Q_{jr}|=s.\n  \\]\n  Choose \\(a_{j1},a_{j2}\\) as equally as possible, summing to \\(k_j\\). Child 1 has counts \\(a_{j1},a_{j2}\\); child 2 has counts \\(s/2-a_{j1},s/2-a_{j2}\\). Each child has two leaves, one using each \\(Q_{jr}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The left parent never uses \\(M_R\\), and the right parent never uses \\(M_L\\). Each leaf has its own disjoint background part.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(r=n-3m\\) and compute\n  \\[\n  b_{0L}=\\left\\lfloor\\log\\binom m{k_0}\\right\\rfloor,\\quad\n  b_{0R}=\\left\\lfloor\\log\\binom m{s-k_0}\\right\\rfloor,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  b_{j1}=\\left\\lfloor\\log\\binom m{k_j}\\right\\rfloor,\\quad\n  b_{j2}=\\left\\lfloor\\log\\binom m{s-k_j}\\right\\rfloor.\n  \\]\n  Assign background counts\n  \\[\n  r_L=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{r+b_{0R}-b_{0L}}2\\right\\rfloor,\\qquad r_R=r-r_L,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  r_{j1}=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{r_j+b_{j2}-b_{j1}}2\\right\\rfloor,\\qquad\n  r_{j2}=r_j-r_{j1}.\n  \\]\n  Split each child’s background equally between its leaves, up to one, using a fixed order of background indices. Fail if any integer feasibility test fails.</p><p class=\"li\">A leaf contains one occurrence for every subset satisfying its prescribed top and local counts and every arbitrary background subset. Store actual weight and mixer masks; enumerate fixed-cardinality subsets directly.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\tau=.0078n,\\quad f_i=\\min(\\tau,d_i/2),\\quad L_i=s-f_i,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  f_{\\max}=\\max_i f_i,\\qquad\n  \\xi=n/4+.032m-f_{\\max}.\n  \\tag{16}\n  \\]\n  Set\n  \\[\n  \\ell_i=\\lfloor L_i\\rfloor\n  =\\max\\left\\{\\lfloor s-\\tau\\rfloor,\\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\log N_i}2\\right\\rfloor\\right\\}.\n  \\]\n  Sort the three \\(\\ell_i\\). For each increment from the preceding value, initially zero, sample an independent prime with that increment as parameter if it is at least four; otherwise use factor one. At each sorted index let \\(p_i\\) be the cumulative product. Then \\(p_L\\mid p_0\\) and \\(p_R\\mid p_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Independently sample \\(q\\in\\mathcal P_{\\ell_q}\\), where\n  \\[\n  \\ell_q=\\left\\lfloor n/4+.032m-s+\\min_i\\ell_i\\right\\rfloor,\n  \\]\n  and put \\(P=p_0q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose independent uniform residues \\(\\rho_0\\bmod p_0\\) and \\(\\rho_j\\bmod p_j\\), \\(j=L,R\\), independently of \\(q\\). Set\n  \\[\n  w_L=\\rho_0,\\qquad w_R=t'-\\rho_0.\n  \\]\n  On side \\(j\\), child 1 must have weight \\(\\rho_j\\bmod p_j\\), and child 2 weight \\(w_j-\\rho_j\\bmod p_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A parent record is \\((x,D)\\), its exact weight and its top mask \\(D\\subseteq M_0\\). Deduplication uses both fields.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(z=0,\\ldots,q-1\\), set\n  \\[\n  v_L=\\rho_0+p_0z,\\qquad v_R=(t'-v_L)\\bmod P.\n  \\]\n  Generate each parent by joining its two low-filtered child streams for total residue \\(v_j\\bmod P\\), rejecting overlap on \\(M_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A left task uses no top-mask prefix restriction, and its capacity-\\(C\\) dictionary causes the bucket to be skipped if overflowing or empty.</p><p class=\"li\">Retain a completed nonempty left array while processing right tasks. A right task initially has empty prefix. If it completes nonempty, apply the final weighted-disjointness check. If it overflows, discard its workspace and process its two next-bit prefix tasks depth first. An overflowing full-mask task is dropped.</p><p class=\"li\">In every prefix task, exclude incompatible leaf items before streaming: each leaf tests precisely the prefix bits in its own top block.</p><p class=\"li\">Separating families for final checks are sampled independently of all parent-generation choices, conditional on the layout, and reused across checks. Their values cannot affect parent generation, except by stopping the trial with YES.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large main-branch inputs:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The background assignments are feasible, every raw leaf list has fewer than \\(2^{.185n}\\) items, and the modular size and collision estimates of Lemma 4.2 hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every retained parent record has a subset witness. Deduplication by weight and top mask preserves every valid final match, and every YES is correct.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The complete hypothetical schedule, continued after YES answers and without an operation cap, obeys the task, raw-occurrence, and final-check accounting in Lemma 4.4.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Its concrete stopped parent tasks have overhead and per-tuple cost\n   \\[\n   H=u^8(2^{.2n}+G),\\qquad K=u^8,\n   \\tag{22}\n   \\]\n   with \\(G\\) defined in Lemma 4.5.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 establishes feasible balanced background assignments and leaf-size bounds. Lemma 4.2 establishes the nested-modulus estimates and compatibility of the child filters. Lemma 4.3 proves witness preservation and one-sided correctness.</p><p>Lemma 4.5 verifies that the concrete early prefix restrictions satisfy the occurrence-label premises of Lemma 4.4 and supplies its stopped-task cost. Applying Lemma 4.4 then gives the three separate controls on task starts, raw tuple processing, and repeated final-check inputs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Balanced leaf sizes, from Section 4.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Balanced leaf sizes, from Section 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the layout and background assignments of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  H_{0L}=h(\\lambda_0),\\quad H_{0R}=h(1/2-\\lambda_0),\n  \\quad \\overline H_0=(H_{0L}+H_{0R})/2,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  H_{j1}=h(\\lambda_j),\\quad H_{j2}=h(1/2-\\lambda_j),\n  \\quad \\overline H_j=(H_{j1}+H_{j2})/2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A raw child pair chooses one occurrence from each of its two leaves. A raw four-leaf tuple chooses one occurrence from each of a parent’s four leaves, retaining multiplicity and local overlaps.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\beta_j=r/4+\\overline H_0m/2+\\overline H_jm.\n  \\tag{14}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large main-branch inputs,\n\\[\nr_j=r/2+(\\overline H_0-H_{0j})m+O(\\log n),\n\\]\n\\[\nr_{ji}=r_j/2+(\\overline H_j-H_{ji})m+O(\\log n).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\nAll assignments are feasible. Also,\n\\[\n\\beta_j\\le n/4+(-.75+1.5h_*)m,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{leaf items on side }j\\}\\le u^{10}2^{\\beta_j/2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{raw pairs for a child on side }j\\}\\le u^{20}2^{\\beta_j}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\nEvery leaf list has fewer than \\(2^{.185n}\\) items.</p>", "proof": "<p>Concavity gives\n\\[\n\\overline H_0,\\overline H_L,\\overline H_R\\le h_*.\n\\]\nThe binomial estimates of Proposition 1 translate the integer background assignments into (13), expressing cancellation between larger prescribed mixer families and fewer unconstrained background indices.</p><p>Every entropy-pair argument lies in \\([1/6,1/3]\\), so its absolute difference is at most\n\\[\n1-h(1/6)&lt;3/8.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nr_j\\ge.14n-\\frac{3m}{16}-O(\\log n)\n\\ge.095n-O(\\log n),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nr_{ji}\\ge\\frac{.095n-.09n}{2}-O(\\log n)&gt;0.\n\\]\nAll prescribed mixer counts are rounded versions of fractions in \\([1/6,1/3]\\), so they are feasible as well.</p><p>Before substituting (13), the logarithm of a leaf size is at most\n\\[\nH_{0j}m/4+H_{ji}m/2+r_{ji}/2+O(1).\n\\]\nThe binomial logarithm errors in each background allocation are at most \\(\\log(n+1)+1\\). A rounded prescribed count changes its entropy exponent by \\(O(1)\\), because for large \\(n\\) all relevant fractions lie in \\([1/8,3/8]\\), where \\(|h'|&lt;3\\).</p><p>More explicitly, the error in \\(r_j\\) from its ideal value is at most \\(3\\log u\\), and the error in \\(r_{ji}\\) after both ideal substitutions is at most \\(5\\log u\\). Substitution yields \\(\\beta_j/2+O(\\log n)\\), covered by \\(u^{10}\\). Multiplying two leaf bounds gives the raw-child-pair bound.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\beta_j/2\\le.181052n\n\\]\nby \\(h_*&lt;.8114\\) and \\(m\\le.24n\\). Since \\(\\log u\\le n/10^9\\), every raw leaf list has fewer than \\(2^{.185n}\\) items for sufficiently large main-branch inputs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Nested-modulus estimates, from Section 4.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Nested-modulus estimates, from Section 4.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(L_i,\\ell_i,p_i,\\xi,\\ell_q,q\\) from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Delta\\) be a fixed nonzero relevant actual-weight difference, conditional on the logarithmic parameters.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac{2^{L_i}}{u^5}\\le p_i\\le2^{L_i},\\qquad\n\\Pr[p_i\\mid\\Delta]\\le u^{10}2^{-L_i}\n\\quad(i=0,L,R).\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\nFurthermore,\n\\[\n\\xi-2&lt;\\ell_q\\le\\xi,\\qquad \\xi\\le.25768n,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{2^\\xi}{u^2}\\le q\\le2^\\xi,\\qquad\n\\Pr[q\\mid\\Delta]\\le u^3 2^{-\\xi}.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\nThese statements do not require sampled primes to be distinct or \\(q\\) to be coprime to \\(p_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At most three factors are sampled in forming the nested \\(p_i\\). Discarded increments total at most nine. Prime lower ranges and the floor therefore lose a factor at most\n\\[\n2^{10}(4n)^3\n\\]\nrelative to \\(2^{L_i}\\), which is at most \\(u^5\\) for large \\(n\\). The upper bound follows from the upper prime ranges.</p><p>A fixed \\(\\Delta\\) has at most \\(2u\\) distinct prime divisors. A factor with parameter \\(\\ell\\) divides it with probability at most \\(8u^2 2^{-\\ell}\\), by Proposition 1. The sampled factors are independent. Divisibility by their product requires divisibility by every sampled factor. Multiplying these bounds, with the discarded-increment and floor losses, gives (17). This argument does not require distinct sampled primes.</p><p>Since\n\\[\n\\min_iL_i=s-f_{\\max},\n\\]\nthe formula for \\(\\ell_q\\) gives \\(\\xi-2&lt;\\ell_q\\le\\xi\\). Also\n\\[\n\\xi\\le n/4+.032m\\le.25768n.\n\\]\nThe parameter \\(\\ell_q\\) is positive and linear in \\(n\\). Applying Proposition 1 to this independent prime gives (18).</p><p>The child moduli divide \\(p_0\\). Thus, if a parent has the required residue modulo \\(p_0\\) and child 1 has its required residue modulo \\(p_j\\), the required residue of child 2 follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the signed transformation, parent records, local disjointness tests, and final top-mask disjointness test of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every retained parent entry has a subset witness on its parent’s indices. Deduplicating by actual weight and top mask preserves the existence of every valid final match. Every YES returned by either trial is correct.</p>", "proof": "<p>Within parent \\(j\\), the children share only \\(M_j\\), and their disjointness there has been checked. Their top blocks and background parts are disjoint. Thus every retained entry has a subset witness.</p><p>The only indices shared between the two parents are in \\(M_0\\). Once their top masks are fixed, the choice among witnesses of a given weight does not affect compatibility with the opposite parent. Deduplication therefore loses no valid final match.</p><p>A final YES has disjoint top masks and actual total weight \\(t'\\), so its witnesses give a signed solution. For every subset \\(I'\\), its signed weight equals the original weight of \\(I'\\triangle F\\) minus the original weight of \\(F\\). Equation (12) therefore gives an original solution. The compressed-block trial is one-sided by Proposition 3.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.2 (Prefix accounting)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix positive integers \\(C,q\\) and a nonnegative integer \\(m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each side \\(j=L,R\\), let \\(\\mathcal T_j\\) be a finite collection of raw tuple occurrences. Every occurrence has one bucket in \\(\\{0,\\ldots,q-1\\}\\) and one top mask in \\(\\{0,1\\}^m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A valid occurrence produces an entry consisting of exact weight and top mask. Occurrences producing the same entry have the same bucket.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal U_j\\) be a finite ambient set containing every distinct valid entry, with consistent bucket and mask labels. Write\n  \\[\n  J_j=|\\mathcal T_j|,\\qquad U_j=|\\mathcal U_j|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A bucket-prefix task generates exactly the raw occurrences with those labels unless stopped early, and overflows at the \\((C+1)\\)st distinct valid entry.</p><p class=\"li\">A task with \\(Z\\) potential occurrences costs at most \\(H+KZ\\), even if stopped partway, for fixed nonnegative \\(H,K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Apply the left-skip and right-prefix schedule of Proposition 4, ignoring early YES answers and the operation cap.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(T\\) is the number of parent-generation tasks, \\(W_{\\mathrm{parent}}\\) their total work, and \\(N_{\\mathrm{check}}\\) the total input-array length across final checks, counting each reuse separately, then\n\\[\nT\\le2q+2(m+1)U_R/C,\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\\[\nW_{\\mathrm{parent}}\n\\le H\\bigl(2q+2(m+1)U_R/C\\bigr)\n+K\\bigl(J_L+(m+1)J_R\\bigr),\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\\[\nN_{\\mathrm{check}}\\le U_L+(2m+3)U_R.\n\\tag{21}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At any fixed prefix depth, different right tasks have disjoint possible entries: buckets are disjoint, and equal-length prefixes partition masks within a bucket. Each overflow witnesses more than \\(C\\) ambient right entries. There are at most \\(U_R/C\\) overflowing nodes at each depth and at most \\((m+1)U_R/C\\) over all depths.</p><p>There are at most \\(q\\) left tasks and \\(q\\) right root tasks. Every nonroot task is one of at most two children of an overflow node. This gives (19), including full-mask overflows dropped without children.</p><p>Each left occurrence has one bucket and appears in at most one left task. Each right occurrence has one bucket and one prefix at each depth, so it is potential in at most \\(m+1\\) tasks. This counts occurrence identities, not only weights or masks. It includes invalid local-overlap tuples because their top masks are defined before the overlap test and incompatible prefixes are excluded before the join.</p><p>Thus total potential occurrences are at most\n\\[\nJ_L+(m+1)J_R.\n\\]\nSumming the stopped-task costs gives (20). Only overhead \\(H\\) is multiplied by the number of tasks.</p><p>Completed right arrays contain disjoint entries. Different buckets are disjoint; within a bucket, completed prefixes are incomparable because a completed task has no descendants. Right-array contributions to \\(N_{\\mathrm{check}}\\) total at most \\(U_R\\).</p><p>Left arrays used at right-root checks total at most \\(U_L\\). Each nonroot check reuses a left array of length at most \\(C\\), and at most \\(2(m+1)U_R/C\\) nonroot tasks exist. Their reused left lengths total at most \\(2(m+1)U_R\\). Adding these contributions gives (21).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Concrete stopped-task cost, from Section 4.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Concrete stopped-task cost, from Section 4.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix mixers, signs, cardinality guesses, layout, low moduli, and low residues in Proposition 4, without fixing \\(q\\) or the separating families.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be the sum over all four children of the numbers of raw leaf pairs satisfying their child low filters, before prefix restrictions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal T_j\\) contain every raw four-leaf occurrence on side \\(j\\) satisfying the parent filter modulo \\(p_0\\) and first-child filter modulo \\(p_j\\), retaining repetitions and local overlaps.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal U_j\\) contain every distinct valid weight–top-mask entry satisfying the parent low filter, ignoring child filters. Validity means that the entry has a subset witness on side \\(j\\)’s indices with prescribed top-block counts.</p><p class=\"li\">For a concrete parent task, let \\(Z\\) count its potential matching raw four-leaf occurrences.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The concrete schedule satisfies the premises of Lemma 4.4. For sufficiently large main-branch inputs, each parent task, even stopped partway, costs at most\n\\[\nH+KZ,\\qquad\nH=u^8(2^{.2n}+G),\\quad K=u^8.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Every raw four-leaf tuple has a well-defined top mask: \\(T_1,\\ldots,T_4\\) partition \\(M_0\\), and each occurs in exactly one leaf of each parent. This remains true if local masks overlap. Testing each prefix bit in its responsible leaf therefore produces exactly the restricted occurrence collection required by Lemma 4.4.</p><p>The dictionary deduplicates only after rejecting local overlap. Equal keys have equal actual weights and hence the same bucket. For every \\(q\\), the classes enumerated modulo \\(P=p_0q\\) partition all low-filtered occurrences and entries; no coprimality is required.</p><p>By Lemma 4.1, four raw leaf arrays in a task have total length\n\\[\nA&lt;4\\cdot2^{.185n}.\n\\]\nRegenerating weights, masks, and prefix tests costs \\(O(n^2A)\\). Sorting and stream initialization cost \\(O(nA)\\). Both child streams together have at most \\(G\\) occurrences. Their arrays and mutable states occupy \\(O(A)\\le B\\) words for large \\(n\\).</p><p>The tie buffer has length \\(B\\). Proposition 2 charges replay, including stopped replay, to stream advances and potential matching pairs. Dictionary initialization costs at most \\(O(u(C+1))\\); local mask checks and trie actions cost \\(O(u)\\) per tuple. Hence task work is\n\\[\nO\\bigl(n^2A+B+nG+u(C+1)+uZ\\bigr).\n\\]\nSince \\(C\\le2^{.199n}\\), \\(B\\le2^{.19n}\\), and \\(\\log u\\le n/10^9\\), this is at most \\(H+KZ\\) for sufficiently large main-branch inputs.</p><p>The raw term includes tuples later rejected for overlap and repeated occurrences. A small \\(U_j\\) does not bound it.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Weighted disjointness with reusable separating families.", "statement": "<p>**Weighted disjointness with reusable separating families. Original labels: Lemma 5.1 and Corollary 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  u=n+b+2,\\quad m=8\\lfloor3n/100\\rfloor,\\quad g=m/4,\\quad\n  C=2^{\\lfloor.199n\\rfloor},\\quad h_*=h(1/4).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Partition a top mixer as\n  \\[\n  M_0=T_1\\dot\\cup\\cdots\\dot\\cup T_4,\\qquad |T_i|=g.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k_0\\in\\{\\lceil m/6\\rceil,\\ldots,m/4\\}\\) and \\(k=k_0/m\\). Write \\(k_0=4d+r\\), \\(0\\le r&lt;4\\), and set \\(c_i=d+1\\) for \\(i\\le r\\), and \\(c_i=d\\) otherwise.</p><p class=\"li\">Each check receives two nonempty arrays \\(\\mathcal L,\\mathcal R\\), each of length at most \\(C\\). A left record is \\((x,D)\\), a right record is \\((y,E)\\), and\n  \\[\n  |D\\cap T_i|=c_i,\\qquad |E\\cap T_i|=g/2-c_i.\n  \\tag{23}\n  \\]\n  Repeated records are allowed. The desired match satisfies\n  \\[\n  D\\cap E=\\varnothing,\\qquad x+y=t'.\n  \\tag{24}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Actual weights and target have magnitude below \\(2^{b+\\lceil\\log(n+2)\\rceil+4}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define exactly\n  \\[\n  \\alpha=\\log3,\\qquad\n  \\alpha_m=\\frac{\\lfloor\\log(3^m)\\rfloor}{m},\\qquad\n  e=\\left\\lfloor g\\left(\\frac12+\\alpha_m\\left(k-\\frac14\\right)\\right)\\right\\rfloor,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\nu_i=\n  \\left\\lceil10\\binom ge\\Big/\\binom{g/2}{e-c_i}\\right\\rceil.\n  \\tag{25}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For each block \\(i\\), sample an indexed family\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F_i=(S_{i,1},\\ldots,S_{i,\\nu_i})\n  \\]\n  of independent uniform \\(e\\)-subsets of \\(T_i\\). All four families are independent; repetitions retain separate indices.</p><p class=\"li\">The finite list of checks, and any designated check and designated solution pair, are independent of these families conditional on their common layout parameters. Equivalently, after conditioning on those data, the families still have the product law just specified.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  N_{\\mathrm{check}}=\\sum_{\\text{checks}}(|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(n\\), there is a one-sided weighted-disjointness procedure with:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Family preprocessing costing \\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)2^{2g}\\) operations, and workspace\n   \\[\n   O(nC+n^2+2^{2g})\n   \\]\n   on every family outcome, including preprocessing.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Conditional expected total check work at most\n   \\[\n   W_0=N_{\\mathrm{check}}u^{22}2^{(1-h_*)m}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For every \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1\\), probability at most \\(\\delta\\) that total check work exceeds \\(W_0/\\delta\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. If a designated solution pair is specified as above, probability at least\n   \\[\n   1-4e^{-10}-\\delta\n   \\]\n   that the pair is covered and total check work is at most \\(W_0/\\delta\\).</p><p>For one fixed pair in one check, coverage probability is at least \\(1-4e^{-10}\\). Bounded family sampling has deterministic cost \\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)2^{2g}\\), or reports failure, and couples to the ideal families with loss \\(\\exp(-\\Omega(n^2))\\). Stopping any traversal early cannot increase its work or space.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 5.1–5.3 prove the single-check assertions of original Lemma 5.1.</p><p>A uniform \\(e\\)-set is generated by taking the first \\(e\\) positions of a uniform block permutation. Across all four families there are at most\n\\[\n4g(10\\cdot2^g+1)\n\\]\nrange calls. Proposition 1 gives aggregate family coupling loss at most\n\\[\n4g(10\\cdot2^g+1)2^{-n^3}\n=\\exp(-\\Omega(n^2)).\n\\]\nAllowing all \\(n^3\\) attempts per draw adds only a polynomial factor, fitting the stated preprocessing bound. The ideal product law is used and coupling loss subtracted, rather than conditioning the law on all bounded draws succeeding.</p><p>For the reusable-family assertion, condition on the complete finite list of checks and its parameters. Lemma 5.3 applies to each input occurrence; linearity of expectation gives\n\\[\nW_0=N_{\\mathrm{check}}u^{22}2^{(1-h_*)m}.\n\\]\nFor an empty list, both sides are zero. Otherwise Markov’s inequality gives probability at most \\(\\delta\\) of exceeding \\(W_0/\\delta\\).</p><p>Condition additionally on a designated check and solution pair. By assumption the family law is unchanged. Lemma 5.1 gives coverage probability at least \\(1-4e^{-10}\\). A union bound gives simultaneous coverage and the work bound with probability at least \\(1-4e^{-10}-\\delta\\); independence of these events is not required. This is original Corollary 5.2.</p><p>In the application, continue parent generation as if checks never return YES and no operation cap exists, while retaining overflow rules. That defines the complete check list without consulting the families. The list and any designated witness chosen from it using only parent-generation choices preserve the required conditional family law. The hypothetical list is never stored.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Coverage argument in original Lemma 5.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Coverage argument in original Lemma 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the block counts, family sizes, and ideal independent families of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a pair \\((x,D),(y,E)\\) satisfying (24), independently of all families.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With probability at least \\(1-4e^{-10}\\), some tuple of family indices separates the pair on all four blocks: on each block, its sampled set contains \\(D\\cap T_i\\) and avoids \\(E\\cap T_i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\n\\zeta=\\frac12+\\alpha\\left(k-\\frac14\\right),\\qquad\nY=\\zeta-k=\\frac14+(\\alpha-1)\\left(k-\\frac14\\right).\n\\]\nThen \\(e=\\zeta g+O(1)\\) and \\(c_i=kg+O(1)\\), uniformly for \\(k\\in[1/6,1/4]\\). Since \\(1&lt;\\alpha&lt;2\\), \\(Y\\) stays strictly between zero and \\(1/2\\), so the binomial arguments are feasible for large \\(n\\).</p><p>On \\(T_i\\), a separating sampled set must include the \\(c_i\\) left indices and exclude the \\(g/2-c_i\\) right indices. Their union has size \\(g/2\\). Its exact separation probability is\n\\[\n\\theta_i=\\binom{g/2}{e-c_i}\\Big/\\binom ge.\n\\]\nBy (25), \\(\\nu_i\\theta_i\\ge10\\). The probability that no member separates the pair on this block is at most\n\\[\n(1-\\theta_i)^{\\nu_i}\\le e^{-10}.\n\\]\nA union bound over four blocks gives the claim. Only coverage of this fixed pair is required, not simultaneous coverage of every pair.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Traversal and deterministic workspace in original Lemma 5.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Traversal and deterministic workspace in original Lemma 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use one check and its sampled families from Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">A left record is compatible with family index \\(j\\) on block \\(i\\) if \\(D\\cap T_i\\subseteq S_{i,j}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A right record is compatible if \\((E\\cap T_i)\\cap S_{i,j}=\\varnothing\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a traversal that finds every pair separated by some tuple of indices unless it has already returned YES. It returns YES only for (24), uses\n\\[\nO(nC+n^2+2^{2g})\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\nwords on every outcome, and preprocesses the families in \\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)2^{2g}\\) operations.</p><p>For a fixed input occurrence, let \\(t_i\\) be its number of compatible indices in family \\(i\\). Its total work charge in a complete traversal is at most\n\\[\nu^4\\sum_{j=0}^4\\prod_{i=1}^j t_i,\n\\tag{27}\n\\]\nwhere the empty product is one.</p>", "proof": "<p>For every block mask, store sorted lists of compatible family indices for containment and avoidance. Since\n\\[\n\\binom{g/2}{e-c_i}\\ge1,\n\\qquad \\nu_i\\le10\\cdot2^g+1,\n\\]\nthe two lists for each mask need at most \\(2\\nu_i\\) words. Across four blocks and \\(2^g\\) masks, a flat contents array of capacity\n\\[\n8\\cdot2^g(10\\cdot2^g+1)\n\\]\nsuffices. Offsets and lengths use constantly many words per mask. Families use \\(O(2^g+n)\\) words, and local-index maps and control data use \\(O(n^2)\\). Total preprocessing storage is \\(O(2^{2g}+n^2)\\), and filling the lists in increasing index order costs \\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)2^{2g}\\). Binary search decides whether a record has any compatible index in a specified interval.</p><p>Process blocks in order. At the current block, begin with interval \\([1,\\nu_i]\\). Filter both received arrays to records having a compatible index in the interval. If either filtered array is empty, stop this branch. Otherwise split a nonsingleton interval into two nearly equal intervals and visit both. At a singleton, fix that index and enter the next block. After all four indices are fixed, sort the arrays by actual weight and search for weights summing to \\(t'\\).</p><p>Every pair reaching a terminal search is disjoint because the left mask lies within each chosen set and the right mask lies outside it. A separated pair remains present along the path to its separating tuple and therefore reaches a terminal exact-weight search unless an earlier YES has occurred.</p><p>Use depth-first traversal and retain parent arrays until children finish. Each depth has its own buffers, written only to current lengths. In particular, no length-\\(C\\) buffer is cleared for a small filtered array. Total active depth is at most\n\\[\n4+\\sum_{i=1}^4\\lceil\\log\\nu_i\\rceil\\le m+20=O(n).\n\\]\nAt each depth retain at most two arrays of length \\(C\\). Terminal sorting needs \\(O(C)\\) extra words, and stack control uses \\(O(n)\\). This proves (26).</p><p>For the work charge, fix an input occurrence. For every compatible prefix of preceding indices, it is scanned at the next block’s root. If \\(t_i=0\\), that scan removes it. If \\(t_i&gt;0\\), the union of paths to its \\(t_i\\) compatible leaves has\n\\[\nO(t_i(1+\\log\\nu_i))\n\\]\nnodes; scans into their children have the same order. There are at most \\(\\prod_{r&lt;i}t_r\\) compatible preceding prefixes. Its scans at block \\(i\\) are therefore bounded by a polynomial factor times\n\\[\n\\left(\\prod_{r&lt;i}t_r\\right)(1+t_i).\n\\]\nThe term one is needed when \\(t_i=0\\).</p><p>An interval predicate costs \\(O(n)\\), including binary search and mask extraction, and each interval tree has depth \\(O(n)\\). Terminal sorting and searching cost \\(O(n)\\) per surviving occurrence. Node overhead is charged to scans because a child or new block is entered only with nonempty arrays. For large \\(n\\), these charges are bounded by (27). Early stopping only reduces work and space.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Expected-incidence estimate and the unnumbered entropy claim (30), in original Lemma 5.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Expected-incidence estimate and the unnumbered entropy claim (30), in original Lemma 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 5 and the compatible-index counts \\(t_i\\) of Lemma 5.2.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(k\\in[1/6,1/3]\\), define\n  \\[\n  X=1-k,\\qquad\n  Y=\\frac14+(\\alpha-1)\\left(k-\\frac14\\right),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  E(k)=Xh(Y/X)-\\frac12h(2Y),\\qquad \\alpha=\\log3.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The exact entropy bound\n\\[\nE(k)\\le1-h_*\n\\qquad(1/6\\le k\\le1/3)\n\\tag{30}\n\\]\nholds. For either side and each block,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E t_i\\le u^4 2^{(1-h_*)g}.\n\\]\nConsequently, a single check has expected work at most\n\\[\n(|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|)u^{22}2^{(1-h_*)m}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For a left mask of size \\(c=c_i\\), containment in a uniform \\(e\\)-set has probability\n\\[\n\\binom{g-c}{e-c}\\Big/\\binom ge.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\nu_i\\le11/\\theta_i\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E t_i\n\\le11\\binom{g-c}{e-c}\\Big/\\binom{g/2}{e-c}.\n\\tag{28}\n\\]\nThe binomial estimates of Proposition 1 show that the logarithm of the right side is at most\n\\[\ngE(k)+O(\\log n).\n\\tag{29}\n\\]\nThis is uniform on \\([1/6,1/3]\\): \\(Y\\) lies between \\(k\\) and \\(1/4\\), while \\(Y/X\\) and \\(2Y\\) stay away from zero and one. Their entropy derivatives are bounded in a fixed neighborhood. Rounding \\(c,e\\) by \\(O(1)\\) changes entropy exponents by only \\(O(1)\\), and the lower binomial estimate for the denominator contributes at most \\(\\log(g/2+1)\\), apart from constants.</p><p>For right records, complement sampled sets and put\n\\[\nk'=\\frac12-k,\\qquad c'=g/2-c,\\qquad e'=g-e.\n\\]\nThe complements are uniform \\(e'\\)-sets with ideal bias\n\\[\n1-\\zeta=\\frac12+\\alpha(k'-1/4).\n\\]\nRounding again costs \\(O(1)\\). Also\n\\[\ne'-c'=g/2-(e-c),\\qquad\n\\binom{g/2}{e'-c'}=\\binom{g/2}{e-c}.\n\\]\nThus (28)–(29) apply with \\(k'\\in[1/4,1/3]\\).</p><p>To prove (30), let\n\\[\nF=(\\ln2)E,\\qquad Z=X-Y,\\qquad W=1/2-Y.\n\\]\nExpanding entropy gives\n\\[\nF=X\\ln X-Z\\ln Z-\\frac12\\ln\\frac12+W\\ln W.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nF'=\\ln\\frac ZX+(\\alpha-1)\\ln\\frac ZW,\n\\]\n\\[\nF''=\\frac1X-\\frac{\\alpha^2}{Z}+\\frac{(\\alpha-1)^2}{W}.\n\\]\nAll denominators are positive even on \\([0,1/2]\\), since\n\\[\nZ=\\frac{2+\\alpha}{4}-\\alpha k,\\qquad\nW=\\frac\\alpha4-(\\alpha-1)k,\\qquad1&lt;\\alpha&lt;2.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac ZW\\right)'=\\frac{\\alpha-2}{4W^2}&lt;0,\n\\]\nso \\(Z/W\\le(2+\\alpha)/\\alpha\\). Since \\(Y&gt;0\\), \\(Z/X\\le1\\). Therefore\n\\[\nZF''\n=\\frac ZX-\\alpha^2+(\\alpha-1)^2\\frac ZW\n\\le1-\\alpha^2+(\\alpha-1)^2\\frac{2+\\alpha}{\\alpha}\n=-2+\\frac2\\alpha&lt;0.\n\\]\nThus \\(E\\) is strictly concave.</p><p>At \\(k=1/4\\), \\(X=3/4,Z=1/2,W=1/4\\), and\n\\[\nF'=\\ln(2/3)+(\\alpha-1)\\ln2=0.\n\\]\nThe maximum is\n\\[\nE(1/4)=\\frac34h(1/3)-\\frac12=1-h(1/4).\n\\]\nThe identity follows from\n\\[\nh(1/3)=\\log3-\\frac23,\\qquad\nh(1/4)=2-\\frac34\\log3.\n\\]\nThis proves (30) without a positive error in the exponential rate.</p><p>Combining (28)–(30) gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E t_i\\le u^4 2^{(1-h_*)g}.\n\\]\nThe four block families are independent, so expectations of products in (27) factor. Since \\(m=4g\\) and \\(1-h_*&gt;0\\), each of the five terms has expected charge at most \\(u^{20}2^{(1-h_*)m}\\). Their sum is at most \\(u^{22}2^{(1-h_*)m}\\) for large \\(n\\). Sum over input occurrences.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Survival before the extra prime and separating families.", "statement": "<p>**Survival before the extra prime and separating families. Original label: Lemma 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The input is a main-branch YES instance.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the representation trial of Proposition 4, with ideal exact sampling.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mu\\) be the mean deficiency of a uniform size-\\(s\\) input domain, and assume\n  \\[\n  \\mu&lt;.00765n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The extra prime \\(q\\) and separating families are not exposed.</p><p class=\"li\">A representation specifies a solution’s two parent choices and their child and leaf choices, satisfying every prescribed cardinality and low modular filter.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large main-branch inputs, with probability at least \\(u^{-100}\\), the spread test passes and there is such a representation. Its parent weights \\(x_L,x_R\\), with\n\\[\nx_L+x_R=t',\n\\]\nand all its masks can be fixed as functions of the nonfamily choices preceding \\(q\\). Conditional on those choices, \\(q\\) still has its prescribed independent prime law, and the separating families retain their prescribed product law with the fixed layout parameters.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 6.1–6.3 follow the solution through balance, favorable half orientations, correct cardinality guesses, favorable fine partitions, sufficiently many occupied low residues, and successful target-residue hits.</p><p>For sufficiently large \\(n\\), their conditional probability lower bounds multiply to at least\n\\[\nu^{-3}\\cdot\\frac12\\cdot\\frac18\\cdot u^{-3}\n\\cdot u^{-25}\\cdot\\frac12\\cdot u^{-48}\n\\ge u^{-100}.\n\\]\nThey are conditional lower bounds, not assertions of unconditional independence.</p><p>The representatives may be selected deterministically for analysis. They are fixed before \\(q\\) and the families, so those later choices retain their independent prescribed laws.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Balancing the solution and its deficiencies, from the proof of original Lemma 6.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Balancing the solution and its deficiencies, from the proof of original Lemma 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an original solution \\(S\\) for analysis.</p><p class=\"li\">Under the uniform sign-flip set \\(F\\), put \\(S'=S\\triangle F\\) and\n  \\[\n  A_i=S'\\cap M_i,\\qquad i=0,L,R.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The event \\(|A_0|=|A_L|=|A_R|=s\\) has probability at least \\((m+1)^{-3}\\). Conditional on this balance event, with probability \\(1-O(1/n)\\), the spread test passes and every selected mixer half and its complement has deficiency at most \\(\\tau=.0078n\\). Given the corresponding unordered pairs of halves, a further conditional probability at least \\(1/8\\) yields\n\\[\nd(A_i)\\le f_i=\\min(\\tau,d_i/2)\n\\quad(i=0,L,R).\n\\tag{31}\n\\]\nThus each \\(A_i\\) has at least \\(2^{L_i}\\) distinct signed subset sums.</p>", "proof": "<p>The set \\(S'=S\\triangle F\\) is a uniform subset of all indices, independently of the mixers. Proposition 1 gives balance probability at least \\((m+1)^{-3}\\).</p><p>This event has the same probability for every pre-order mixer triple. Conditional on it, the mixer distribution is unchanged, and selected halves are independent uniform halves given the mixers. Every selected half and complement in a pre-order mixer is marginally a uniform size-\\(s\\) input domain.</p><p>By Proposition 1 and \\(\\mu&lt;.00765n\\), all six halves have deficiency at most \\(\\tau\\) with conditional probability \\(1-O(1/n)\\): a deviation of \\(.0001n\\) above the common mean suffices. The size-\\(m\\) variance bound also shows that every pre-order mixer deficiency lies within \\(.0002n\\) of the size-\\(m\\) mean, with failure \\(O(1/n)\\). Hence the spread test passes jointly for large \\(n\\).</p><p>If \\(A,B\\) partition a mixer \\(M\\) into halves, then\n\\[\nN(M)\\le N(A)N(B),\n\\qquad d(A)+d(B)\\le d(M).\n\\]\nAt least one half has deficiency at most \\(d(M)/2\\).</p><p>Given the three unordered half pairs, each orientation as the selected half is uniform, independently across mixers. The preceding good events and mixer ordering concern original-weight deficiencies of unordered halves and are orientation-invariant. With further conditional probability at least \\(1/8\\), all selected halves satisfy (31). No conditional independence of the signs is asserted or needed.</p><p>Deficiency is sign-invariant, so\n\\[\nN(A_i)\\ge2^{s-f_i}=2^{L_i}\n\\]\nalso for signed weights.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Representative families with prescribed leaf counts, from original Lemma 6.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Representative families with prescribed leaf counts, from original Lemma 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 6 and condition on the favorable events of Lemma 6.1.</p><p class=\"li\">For each mixer, choose analysis representatives before the cardinality guesses and fine subblock partitions.</p><p class=\"li\">A smaller mask is a subset \\(D_i\\subseteq A_i\\); its complement in \\(A_i\\) is assigned to the opposite parent for \\(i=0\\), or opposite child for \\(i=L,R\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each \\(i\\), there is a representative size in\n\\[\n\\{\\lceil m/6\\rceil,\\ldots,m/4\\}\n\\]\nwith at least \\(2^{L_i}/(s+1)\\) distinct signed weights. The three uniform guesses hit chosen sizes with probability at least \\((m+1)^{-3}\\).</p><p>With further probability at least \\(u^{-25}\\), the fine partitions retain representative families \\(X_i\\) of distinct signed weights with associated masks satisfying all prescribed leaf counts and\n\\[\n|X_i|\\ge\\frac{2^{L_i}}{u^{14}}.\n\\tag{32}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Partition the distinct sums on \\(A_i\\) by subset size. Some size has at least \\(2^{L_i}/(s+1)\\) distinct sums. By complementation in \\(A_i\\), choose a maximizing size at most \\(s/2=m/4\\), since complementation preserves the number of distinct sums between complementary sizes.</p><p>For sufficiently large \\(n\\), this size exceeds \\(m/6\\). Otherwise its number of subsets is at most \\(2^{s h(1/3)}\\), whereas\n\\[\n\\frac{s-\\tau-\\log(s+1)}s\\longrightarrow.935\n&gt;\\frac{14}{15}&gt;h(1/3).\n\\]\nChoose one subset for each distinct sum of that size. The three independent guesses hit these sizes with probability at least \\((m+1)^{-3}\\).</p><p>Fix one representative \\(D\\subseteq A_i\\). Let \\(l=4\\) for \\(M_0\\) and \\(l=2\\) for either local mixer. In a uniform partition into \\(l\\) labeled equal blocks, prescribe counts for the three categories\n\\[\nD,\\qquad A_i\\setminus D,\\qquad M_i\\setminus A_i.\n\\]\nIn a top block these are\n\\[\nc_a,\\quad g/2-c_a,\\quad g/2;\n\\]\nin a local block they are\n\\[\na_{jr},\\quad s/2-a_{jr},\\quad s/2.\n\\]\nThey fill each block exactly. Divisibility of \\(m\\) by eight makes complementary counts integral. Each category’s count vector is as equal as possible.</p><p>For \\(v\\) objects, a most balanced multinomial coefficient is maximal: transferring an object from a count to a count smaller by at least two increases the coefficient. There are at most \\((v+1)^l\\) count vectors, whose coefficients sum to \\(l^v\\). Each favorable coefficient is therefore at least \\(l^v/(v+1)^l\\). Their product, divided by the whole-partition coefficient, which is at most \\(l^m\\), shows that \\(D\\) survives with probability at least\n\\[\n(m+1)^{-3l}\\ge u^{-3l}.\n\\]\nSurvival enforces the finer counts for \\(D\\) and its complement.</p><p>If \\(Z\\in[0,1]\\) is a surviving fraction and \\(\\mathbb EZ\\ge p\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb EZ\\le p/2+\\Pr[Z\\ge p/2],\n\\]\nso \\(\\Pr[Z\\ge p/2]\\ge p/2\\). Apply this with \\(p=u^{-3l}\\). The three fine partitions are independent, giving simultaneous retained fractions with probability at least\n\\[\nu^{-24}/8\\ge u^{-25}.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(n\\), the retained distinct signed-weight families satisfy (32).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Low-residue coverage, from original Lemma 6.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Low-residue coverage, from original Lemma 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the favorable setup and representative families \\(X_i\\) from Lemma 6.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Expose the nested prime factors before the independent target residues.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose representatives deterministically whenever an exposed residue hits an occupied class.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With probability at least \\(1-3/u\\) over the nested moduli, each \\(X_i\\) occupies at least \\(p_i/u^{16}\\) residue classes modulo \\(p_i\\). Conditional on such moduli, the residues \\(\\rho_0,\\rho_L,\\rho_R\\), exposed in that order, yield compatible top and child choices with probability at least \\(u^{-48}\\).</p><p>These choices fix a complete surviving representation before exposing \\(q\\) or the separating families.</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(X_i\\), the expected sum of squares of its residue-class sizes modulo \\(p_i\\) is at most\n\\[\n|X_i|+|X_i|^2u^{10}2^{-L_i}\n\\le u^{15}|X_i|^2 2^{-L_i},\n\\]\nusing Proposition 4 and (32).</p><p>Markov’s inequality and a union bound imply, with probability at least \\(1-3/u\\), that all three sums of squares are at most\n\\[\nu^{16}|X_i|^2 2^{-L_i}.\n\\]\nThe moduli need not be independent. Cauchy–Schwarz then gives at least\n\\[\n\\frac{2^{L_i}}{u^{16}}\\ge\\frac{p_i}{u^{16}}\n\\tag{33}\n\\]\noccupied residues.</p><p>Expose \\(\\rho_0\\) first. Assign a surviving smaller mask \\(D_0\\subseteq A_0\\) to the left and its complement in \\(A_0\\) to the right. As \\(D_0\\) varies, the left solution-parent weight is its weight plus a fixed term consisting of the selected left background weight and the weight of \\(A_L\\). By (33), a uniform \\(\\rho_0\\bmod p_0\\) hits such a weight with probability at least \\(u^{-16}\\). On a hit, fix a representative deterministically, fixing disjoint top masks and weights \\(x_L,x_R=t'-x_L\\).</p><p>Splitting \\(A_j\\) between its two children cannot change parent \\(j\\)’s weight, since their union uses all of \\(A_j\\). Assign a surviving smaller mask \\(D_j\\subseteq A_j\\) to child 1 and the complement to child 2. The first-child weight is the weight of \\(D_j\\) plus a now fixed background and top-mask term.</p><p>Expose independent \\(\\rho_L,\\rho_R\\). Both hit possible first-child residues with probability at least \\(u^{-32}\\). The second-child filters follow from \\(p_j\\mid p_0\\) and the parent filters. All leaf counts hold, and background choices have no cardinality constraint. The combined residue probability is at least \\(u^{-48}\\).</p><p>Every representative selection uses only choices preceding \\(q\\) and the families. For any subsequent \\(q\\), one loop iteration has\n\\[\nv_L=x_L\\bmod P,\\qquad v_R=x_R\\bmod P.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Representation survival within the time budget.", "statement": "<p>**Representation survival within the time budget. Original label: Lemma 6.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the main-branch representation trial of Proposition 4 and the weighted-disjointness procedure of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">The input is a YES instance with size-\\(s\\) mean deficiency \\(\\mu&lt;.00765n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All samplers use the bounded implementations of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Abort the trial after\n  \\[\n  u^{500}2^{\\lceil n/2\\rceil}\n  \\]\n  underlying word operations; sampling failure also aborts it.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large main-branch inputs, the representation trial succeeds with probability at least \\(u^{-110}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 6 and Lemma 7.1 give probability at least \\(.9u^{-100}\\) of joint representation survival and resource bounds. Lemma 7.3 gives the bucket event with further conditional probability at least \\(.9\\). Lemma 7.4 gives coverage and bounded check work with conditional probability at least \\(1-4e^{-10}-u^{-10}\\).</p><p>On this intersection, parent generation costs at most \\(u^{420}2^{n/2}\\), and all checks cost at most \\(u^{240}2^{n/2}\\).</p><p>Remaining setup work is smaller. Counting the three mixer sumsets by half-list streams costs \\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)2^m\\). There are constantly many bounded prime calls, with at most \\(n^3\\) attempts each and \\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)2^{.129n}\\) work per attempt. Family sampling and incidence construction cost \\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)2^{2g}\\), including bounded range draws. Direct combination generation and weight/mask formation cost \\(O(n^2)\\) per item; sorting, heap operations, and interval searches cost \\(O(n)\\) per item or advance; trie actions cost \\(O(u)\\) per key.</p><p>These operations fit the displayed polynomial powers. Including worst permitted sampler costs, the favorable execution uses fewer than\n\\[\nu^{500}2^{\\lceil n/2\\rceil}\n\\]\nunderlying word operations for sufficiently large \\(n\\), and hence completes before the cap.</p><p>Proposition 1 couples bounded choices to the ideal experiment with loss \\(\\exp(-\\Omega(n^2))\\) per trial. Since \\(\\log u\\le n/10^9\\), this is smaller than any fixed multiple of \\(u^{-110}\\) for large \\(n\\). Subtracting it from\n\\[\n.81u^{-100}(1-4e^{-10}-u^{-10})\n\\]\nleaves at least \\(u^{-110}\\). Earlier YES answers are already successes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Unconditional first moments and joint resource event, from Section 6.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Unconditional first moments and joint resource event, from Section 6.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 4, but this lemma does not condition on the rare survival event of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Condition on a passing mixer setup, signs, guesses, layout, and allowed low moduli, leaving \\(\\rho_0,\\rho_L,\\rho_R\\) independent and uniform.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G,J_j,U_j\\) be the counts defined in Lemma 4.5.</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\overline H_0,\\beta_j\\) from Lemma 4.1 and define\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma=\\frac n4+\\left(-\\frac54+\\frac32h_*\\right)m+f_{\\max},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  d_*=\\min(d_L,d_R),\\qquad\n  \\Lambda=\\frac n2+(\\overline H_0-1)m-d_*+f_0.\n  \\tag{34}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the resource event\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal E=\n  \\left\\{\n  G\\le u^{200}2^\\Gamma,\\ \n  J_L+J_R\\le u^{200}2^{n/2},\\\n  U_L,U_R\\le u^{200}2^\\Lambda\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\tag{37}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb EG\\le u^{26}2^\\Gamma,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E(J_L+J_R)\\le u^{51}2^{n/2},\\qquad\n\\mathbb EU_j\\le u^{10}2^\\Lambda.\n\\tag{35}\n\\]\nAfter averaging over the conditioning,\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\text{spread test passes and }\\mathcal E^c]\n\\le u^{-174}+u^{-149}+2u^{-190}\n&lt;\\frac1{10}u^{-100}.\n\\tag{38}\n\\]\nConsequently, the survival event of Proposition 6 and \\(\\mathcal E\\) hold jointly with probability at least \\(.9u^{-100}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Each child’s required residue is marginally uniform modulo \\(p_j\\), including child 2 because \\(\\rho_j\\) is independent of \\(\\rho_0\\). Each fixed raw four-leaf tuple satisfies its parent and first-child filters with probability \\(1/(p_0p_j)\\). The second-child filter then follows from \\(p_j\\mid p_0\\).</p><p>Before its parent filter, the number of distinct valid entries on side \\(j\\) is at most\n\\[\n2^{r_j}2^{H_{0j}m}2^{m-d_j}.\n\\]\nThese factors count background choices, possible top masks, and distinct local-mixer weights; the last factor is \\(N_j\\), also after sign changes. Each entry satisfies the parent filter with probability \\(1/p_0\\).</p><p>For \\(G\\), the raw-child bound and modulus lower bound in Proposition 4 give the first estimate because\n\\[\n\\beta_j-L_j\\le\\Gamma,\n\\]\nwith factors \\(u^{20}\\) for raw pairs and \\(u^5\\) for the modulus loss, and room for summing children.</p><p>For \\(J_L+J_R\\), raw tuple counts are at most \\(u^{40}2^{2\\beta_j}\\), while\n\\[\n2\\beta_j-L_0-L_j\n\\le\\frac n2+\\left(-\\frac52+3h_*\\right)m+2\\tau\n\\le\\frac n2.\n\\tag{36}\n\\]\nThe excess is at most \\(-.0658m+.0156n\\), which is negative for large \\(n\\), since \\(m=.24n+O(1)\\). Including both modulus losses and the two sides gives \\(u^{51}2^{n/2}\\).</p><p>For \\(U_j\\), the exponential part of its universe count divided by the parent modulus is\n\\[\nr/2+\\overline H_0m+m-d_j-L_0\n=\\frac n2+(\\overline H_0-1)m-d_j+f_0\n\\le\\Lambda.\n\\]\nAllocation logarithmic errors and the modulus loss fit into \\(u^{10}\\).</p><p>Under the conditioning, all scales are fixed. Apply Markov’s inequality to the four bounds in \\(\\mathcal E\\), then a union bound and averaging. This proves (38). It is an unconditional joint failure estimate, not an estimate conditioned on rare representation survival. Subtracting it from Proposition 6 gives joint probability at least \\(.9u^{-100}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Exponent relations and complete-schedule work, from Sections 6.2 and 6.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Exponent relations and complete-schedule work, from Sections 6.2 and 6.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the passing setup, scales, counts, and event \\(\\mathcal E\\) of Lemma 7.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\xi=n/4+.032m-f_{\\max}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix any allowed extra prime \\(q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Continue the entire parent schedule after YES answers and ignore the operation cap, retaining all overflow rules.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large main-branch inputs,\n\\[\n\\xi+\\Gamma\\le n/2,\\qquad\n\\Lambda+(1-h_*)m\\le n/2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\xi-(\\Lambda-.199n)\\ge.0015n.\n\\tag{39}\n\\]\nOn \\(\\mathcal E\\), the complete schedule satisfies\n\\[\nT\\le u^{203}2^\\xi,\\qquad\nW_{\\mathrm{parent}}\\le u^{420}2^{n/2},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nN_{\\mathrm{check}}\\le U_L+(2m+3)U_R.\n\\tag{43}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first relation in (39), \\(f_{\\max}\\) cancels, and\n\\[\n.032-1.25+1.5h_*&lt;0.\n\\]\nFor the second, use \\(\\overline H_0\\le h_*\\) and \\(f_0\\le d_*\\), since \\(M_0\\) has least deficiency.</p><p>For the third, direct subtraction yields the lower bound\n\\[\n-.051n+(1.032-h_*)m+d_*-f_0-f_{\\max}.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\nf_0\\le d_*/2,\n\\]\nand the spread test gives\n\\[\nf_{\\max}\\le d_*/2+.00025n.\n\\]\nSince \\(1.032-h_*&gt;.2206\\) and \\(m=.24n+O(1)\\), the lower bound is at least \\(.0015n\\) for large \\(n\\). The first two relations retain the exact time exponent \\(1/2\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 4’s prefix accounting. Since \\(q\\le2^\\xi\\), \\(C\\ge2^{.199n-1}\\), and\n\\[\nU_R/C\\le2u^{200}2^{\\xi-.0015n},\n\\]\nthe task count satisfies\n\\[\nT\\le2q+2(m+1)U_R/C\\le u^{203}2^\\xi.\n\\tag{41}\n\\]</p><p>With\n\\[\nH=u^8(2^{.2n}+G),\\qquad K=u^8,\n\\tag{42}\n\\]\nthe work bound gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nW_{\\mathrm{parent}}\n&amp;\\le HT+K(J_L+(m+1)J_R)\\\\\n&amp;\\le u^{211}2^\\xi(2^{.2n}+u^{200}2^\\Gamma)\n+u^8(J_L+(m+1)J_R)\\\\\n&amp;\\le u^{420}2^{n/2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere \\(\\xi+.2n&lt;n/2\\) follows from \\(\\xi\\le.25768n\\), while \\(\\xi+\\Gamma\\le n/2\\) is exact. Raw matches are charged by prefix depth, never multiplied by the number \\(T\\) of tasks.</p><p>The final prefix-accounting bound gives (43). Completed right chunks pay for their own entries, and overflow entries pay for repeated left arrays. Thus final checks use distinct-entry counts \\(U_j\\), although generation required occurrence counts \\(J_j\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**The extra prime and the solution bucket, from Section 6.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**The extra prime and the solution bucket, from Section 6.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Condition on a representation surviving as in Proposition 6 and on \\(\\mathcal E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Expose all preceding choices, but not the independent extra prime \\(q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let the fixed solution-parent weights be \\(x_L,x_R\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large main-branch inputs, with conditional probability at least \\(.9\\) over \\(q\\), the solution’s left bucket completes without overflow, and the solution’s right-prefix path reaches a completed nonoverflow task containing its right entry.</p><p>Consequently, in the complete hypothetical schedule, a final check contains the fixed solution pair.</p>", "proof": "<p>For side \\(j\\), any different actual weight congruent to \\(x_j\\) modulo \\(P=p_0q\\) has a nonzero difference divisible by \\(q\\). Proposition 4’s independent-prime collision bound gives expected different-weight entries at most\n\\[\nU_j u^3 2^{-\\xi}\n\\le u^{203}2^{.199n-.0015n}.\n\\tag{40}\n\\]\nNo coprimality between \\(q,p_0\\) is needed.</p><p>Since \\(C\\ge2^{.199n-1}\\), dividing by \\(C/2\\) gives at most\n\\[\n2^{(203/10^9-.0015)n+2}.\n\\]\nMarkov’s inequality and a union bound imply that both sides have at most \\(C/2\\) different-weight entries with conditional probability at least \\(.9\\) for large \\(n\\).</p><p>Equal-weight entries require a separate argument. On the left there are at most\n\\[\n\\binom m{k_0}\\le2^{h_*m}\\le2^{.194736n}&lt;C/2\n\\]\ndistinct records of weight \\(x_L\\), because their top masks have size at most \\(m/4\\). Thus the complete left solution bucket fits.</p><p>On the right, follow the solution top-mask prefix. A full prefix leaves at most one record of weight \\(x_R\\), by deduplication. Together with the different-weight bound, that full-prefix bucket fits. Therefore the solution path ends at a completed nonoverflow task.</p><p>In every compatible task, the surviving solution leaf choices generate the required parent entry. Hence the pair reaches a final check in the hypothetical schedule; in an actual execution, an earlier YES is already success.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Independent separating families and bounded check work, from Section 6.5.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Independent separating families and bounded check work, from Section 6.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix all choices through \\(q\\) on the simultaneous survival, resource, and bucket events of Lemmas 7.1 and 7.3.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the complete hypothetical list of checks without consulting the separating families.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose deterministically the completed check on the solution-prefix path and its solution pair, using only nonfamily choices.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Conditional on these data, the families retain the product law of Proposition 5. With conditional probability at least\n\\[\n1-4e^{-10}-u^{-10},\n\\]\nthe designated pair is covered and total check work is at most \\(u^{240}2^{n/2}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The complete check list, layout parameters, designated check, and pair are functions of nonfamily choices. Conditional on the layout, the families were sampled independently of every parent and filter choice. Their product law therefore remains unchanged after conditioning jointly on these data. This verifies the reusable-family hypothesis of Proposition 5.</p><p>By Lemma 7.2 and \\(\\mathcal E\\),\n\\[\nN_{\\mathrm{check}}u^{22}2^{(1-h_*)m}\n\\le u^{230}2^{\\Lambda+(1-h_*)m}\n\\le u^{230}2^{n/2}.\n\\]\nMarkov’s inequality gives probability at most \\(u^{-10}\\) of exceeding \\(u^{240}2^{n/2}\\). Coverage has probability at least \\(1-4e^{-10}\\). A union bound gives the simultaneous assertion; the events need not be independent.</p><p>The hypothetical list is only an analysis device and is never stored. The actual run visits only an initial portion of this work.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Uniform finite-word implementation and deterministic resource enforcement.", "statement": "<p>**Uniform finite-word implementation and deterministic resource enforcement. Original material: Sections 7.2–7.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the word model and input parameters of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix one sufficiently large absolute \\(n_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Test the main-branch conditions\n  \\[\n  n\\ge n_0,\\qquad u\\le2^{\\lfloor n/10^9\\rfloor}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Outside the main branch, enumerate all indexed subsets exactly.</p><p class=\"li\">On the main branch, perform \\(u^{120}\\) rounds. In each round, run the compressed-block trial and then the representation trial sequentially, using fresh randomness and reset state.</p><p class=\"li\">Abort each trial after \\(u^{500}2^{\\lceil n/2\\rceil}\\) underlying word operations. Sampling failure also aborts that trial.</p><p class=\"li\">Stop at the first YES. If all rounds finish without YES, return NO.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>This schedule is one finite uniform program, independent of every polynomial-bit exponent \\(c\\). It is exactly correct on the exceptional branch and returns no incorrect YES anywhere.</p><p>All required arithmetic, counters, addresses, masks, and retained random choices have a finite implementation in the stated model. On every main-branch random outcome, simultaneous working storage is\n\\[\nO(u^{15}2^{.199n}),\n\\tag{44}\n\\]\nand total time is\n\\[\nO\\bigl(u^{620}2^{\\lceil n/2\\rceil}+\\operatorname{poly}(u)\\bigr).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 8.1 provides exact finite-word arithmetic and counted randomness. Lemma 8.2 gives deterministic simultaneous space, including transient overflow state, retained arrays, and cleanup. Lemma 8.3 provides a single finite uniform guard, an exact exceptional branch, and deterministic time enforcement.</p><p>Propositions 3 and 4 establish one-sided correctness for all YES answers. The exceptional branch is exact. Together these give the implementation and resource assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Exact word operations, from Section 7.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Exact word operations, from Section 7.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the main branch, word size \\(w\\), trial parameters, and operation cap of Proposition 8.</p><p class=\"li\">Signed integers are represented by sign and magnitude, with canonical zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>All arithmetic invoked as a unit-cost operation in the constructions stays within the stated word model. Actual signed weights, transformed target, and relevant differences have magnitude less than\n\\[\n2^{b+\\lceil\\log(n+2)\\rceil+4}.\n\\]\nAll masks, binomial computations, modulus parameters, residue arithmetic, sampler ranges, fuel counters, and round counters can be represented with the stated bounded word widths.</p>", "proof": "<p>Scan the input and count magnitude bits by shifts to obtain \\(b\\). An invalid parent tuple uses an input index at most twice, so the displayed magnitude bound applies even before disjointness checks. Implement signed addition, comparison, and normalized remainders on sign and magnitude fields. Reduce an actual weight before combining it with a residue. The trie key consists of a fixed-width signed weight followed by relevant mask bits.</p><p>Each mask has at most \\(n\\) bits, and each stored item has constantly many word fields. Binomial coefficients on at most \\(n\\) elements can be computed multiplicatively; immediately before division, the intermediate integer is at most \\(n2^n\\). Floor logarithms are bit lengths. Thus background allocations, modulus parameters, and rational floors are exact integer calculations.</p><p>The separator integer \\(3^m\\) also fits in a word. Feasibility tests precede unsigned subtraction, or the quantities carry a sign. No entropy or real-arithmetic oracle is used.</p><p>The largest product modulus satisfies\n\\[\nP\\le2^{s+.258n}.\n\\]\nResidue arithmetic fits in a word. Trial division compares a divisor with the corresponding quotient rather than unnecessarily forming its square. Range-sampling masks use fewer than \\(w\\) bits.</p><p>The main-branch guard gives\n\\[\n\\log\\!\\left(u^{500}2^{\\lceil n/2\\rceil}\\right)\n\\le(1/2+500/10^9)n+1&lt;w\n\\]\nfor large \\(n\\). Fuel, the \\(u^{120}\\) round count, intermediate powers, timestamps, and counters therefore have bounded width. Memory addresses fit by Lemma 8.2’s space calculation.</p><p>Permutations, sign masks, moduli, residues, family masks, and replay state remain in counted workspace whenever reused. Discarded random values are never read again.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Space on every outcome, from Section 7.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Space on every outcome, from Section 7.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the main-branch implementation of Proposition 8.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  L_{\\max}=2^{.185n},\n  \\]\n  an upper bound on every raw representation leaf list.</p><p class=\"li\">Dictionaries stop at the first key exceeding capacity \\(C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Overflowing right-task workspace is discarded before processing descendants.</p><p class=\"li\">Final checks use the depth-first traversal of Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For an absolute implementation constant \\(K\\), simultaneous writable storage is at most\n\\[\nK\\left[u(C+1)+(n+1)C+L_{\\max}+B+2^{2g}+u^2\\right].\n\\]\nConsequently, every outcome, including overflowing and aborted trials, satisfies (44). Its compact memory addresses fit in one word on the main branch.</p>", "proof": "<p>There are only eight representation leaf lists. Items, heap cells, and stream-row states use constantly many words. Sorting uses linear auxiliary arrays. Stored leaves, heaps, row states, and constantly many replay snapshots therefore occupy \\(O(L_{\\max})\\) words. The tie buffer uses \\(O(B)\\).</p><p>Initial compressed-block lists and mixer-counting half lists have size\n\\[\n2^s\\le2^{.12n};\n\\]\nthe compressed trial’s stored sublists have size at most \\(2^{.14n}\\). These fit the same bound. The two trials run sequentially.</p><p>A dictionary has at most \\(C\\) retained records and stops at a \\((C+1)\\)st key. Its keys have \\(O(u)\\) bits, so its trie, including transient nodes for the rejected key, has \\(O(u(C+1))\\) nodes, each with constantly many fields. Only constantly many dictionaries and parent arrays coexist.</p><p>In right-prefix refinement, discard overflowing workspace before descendants. An \\(O(n)\\)-depth stack stores prefixes and return information, not earlier dictionaries. Retain only the completed left array while the current right task or final check runs.</p><p>The final check’s interval trees have total active depth \\(O(n)\\). Each depth retains constantly many arrays of length at most \\(C\\), giving \\(O(nC)\\) actual record buffers, including linear sorting workspace. Caller-retained parent arrays add \\(O(C)\\), already covered.</p><p>Incidence contents have capacity at most\n\\[\n8\\cdot2^g(10\\cdot2^g+1)=O(2^{2g}).\n\\]\nA family index uses at most \\(g+4\\) bits, and a family mask uses \\(g\\) bits, so each fits in one word. Directories and sampled families add \\(O(2^g)\\), and\n\\[\n2^{2g}\\le2^{.12n}.\n\\]</p><p>Combination generation, permutations, sampler state, parameters, prefix and interval control, counters, and conservative binomial workspace use \\(O(u^2)\\) further words. No hypothetical check list is stored.</p><p>Combining the terms gives the stated explicit bound. Since\n\\[\nC,L_{\\max},B,2^{2g}\\le2^{.199n},\\qquad n\\le u,\n\\]\ntermwise it is\n\\[\nO(u^{15}2^{.199n}).\n\\]\nNo resource event or average-bucket assumption is used.</p><p>Its address bit length is at most\n\\[\n.199n+15\\log u+O(1)&lt;w.\n\\]\nReset allocation and stack pointers between tasks and trials, initializing entries when reused. Even linear clearing is below a trial’s time budget, so aborts accumulate neither data nor uncharged cleanup.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite threshold test and deterministic time enforcement, from Section 7.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Finite threshold test and deterministic time enforcement, from Section 7.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the full schedule of Proposition 8.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose the absolute \\(n_0\\) to meet all sufficiently-large conditions established above.</p><p class=\"li\">The fixed finite program, but no input-dependent advice, is uncharged.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The main-branch guard and exact exceptional routine can be implemented by one finite program even when a small input’s word cannot store \\(n_0\\). The exceptional routine uses an \\(n\\)-bit subset counter and \\(O(n)\\) words. Main-branch operation counters give per-trial time\n\\[\nO(u^{500}2^{\\lceil n/2\\rceil})\n\\]\non every outcome and total schedule time\n\\[\nO(u^{620}2^{\\lceil n/2\\rceil}+\\operatorname{poly}(u)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>There are finitely many sufficiently-large conditions, with absolute constants and fixed polynomial degrees. The guard \\(\\log u\\le n/10^9\\) makes each fixed exponential margin dominate the needed polynomial factors uniformly in the main branch. Choose one absolute \\(n_0\\) satisfying all conditions and fix it in the program independently of \\(c\\).</p><p>Small-word inputs need not store \\(n_0\\). Place an exact subset-enumeration routine near the start of the finite program. Unroll the binary digits of \\(n_0\\), least to most significant, using only literals zero and one. Maintain a shifted copy of \\(n\\) and a three-valued comparison state. At each digit, compare its low bit with the threshold digit, replace the comparison state if they differ, and shift the copy right. Newly seen differences have higher significance.</p><p>If the copy becomes zero before the threshold’s leading digit has been processed, branch to the nearby exhaustive routine. After that leading digit, a nonzero remaining copy means \\(n&gt;n_0\\); otherwise the comparison state decides.</p><p>A short input executes only \\(O(1+\\log n)\\) adjacent digit stages. Its instruction addresses and state remain small, and it never loads the full threshold or a distant main-program address. Enlarge \\(n_0\\), if necessary, so that for \\(n\\ge n_0\\) every other fixed literal and main-program address fits. The second guard is then an integer comparison.</p><p>Both exceptional cases use exact subset enumeration with an \\(n\\)-bit subset counter and \\(O(n)\\) words. This defines one finite program with no advice and no supplied exponent \\(c\\).</p><p>Instrument each trial with a counter for underlying word operations. Counter overhead, stopping, and reset are constant-factor costs; Lemmas 8.1–8.2 bound their representation and cleanup. Input scanning and guards cost polynomial time in \\(u\\). With \\(u^{120}\\) rounds and two sequential capped trials per round, total main-branch time is\n\\[\nO(u^{620}2^{\\lceil n/2\\rceil}+\\operatorname{poly}(u)).\n\\]</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 11, "cite"], [2, 12, "cite"], [2, 14, "cite"], [2, 15, "cite"], [2, 19, "cite"], [2, 22, "cite"], [2, 24, "cite"], [2, 27, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [3, 12, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 2, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [7, 11, "cite"], [7, 18, "cite"], [8, 2, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 2, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [10, 0, "cite"], [10, 16, "cite"], [10, 32, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [13, 23, "cite"], [13, 26, "cite"], [13, 27, "cite"], [13, 28, "cite"], [13, 29, "cite"], [13, 30, "cite"], [13, 32, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [14, 18, "cite"], [14, 28, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 13, "cite"], [17, 13, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [18, 13, "cite"], [18, 28, "cite"], [19, 27, "cite"], [19, 31, "cite"], [19, 34, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [20, 22, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 19, "cite"], [23, 27, "cite"], [23, 28, "cite"], [23, 30, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [24, 26, "cite"], [25, 23, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [25, 27, "cite"], [26, 23, "cite"], [27, 0, "cite"], [28, 27, "cite"], [28, 29, "cite"], [28, 31, "cite"], [29, 31, "cite"], [30, 27, "cite"], [30, 31, "cite"], [31, 27, "cite"], [32, 0, "cite"], [33, 32, "cite"], [33, 35, "cite"], [34, 32, "cite"], [34, 33, "cite"], [34, 35, "cite"], [35, 32, "cite"]]});
